window.TEI_DATA = {
  LESSONS: [{"id": "1.1.1", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Money Mindset", "keyTakeaway": "Your money habits were learned, not chosen.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to recognise your own inherited beliefs about money and identify at least one pattern that shapes how you handle it today.", "prerequisitesNote": "None. This is the first lesson after your Investor Learning Commitment.", "concept": "Most of what you believe about money, you did not choose. You learned it, the same way you learned how to greet people or what a fair argument sounds like: by watching, long before you were old enough to question it.\n\nMaybe money was something spoken about openly in your house, or maybe it was something nobody mentioned at all. Maybe it felt scarce, always slightly at risk. Maybe it felt safe and abundant, rarely worth worrying over. None of these starting points are wrong. They are simply where you began.\n\nThis matters because those early patterns do not stay in childhood. They quietly shape decisions you make as an adult: what you save, what you spend without thinking, what makes you anxious, what makes you feel in control. Before this platform asks you to learn anything about investing, it asks you to notice this first, because the rest of what you learn will be filtered through it whether you notice it or not.", "context": "You are at the very beginning of Stage 1: Foundation. Nothing about investing has been introduced yet, and that is deliberate. The Curriculum is built so that you understand your own financial starting point clearly before adding anything more complex on top of it. Money mindset comes first because it is the lens everything else in this module, and this platform, gets viewed through. Every later lesson on saving, systems, and eventually investing will be filtered through the same instincts this lesson is asking you to notice now.", "example": "Consider two people, raised in very different households.\n\nOne grew up in a home where money was discussed as something fragile: bills paid carefully, treats rare, a general sense that things could go wrong. As an adult, this person finds saving comes naturally, almost compulsively, but spending on anything for themselves feels uncomfortable, even when they can genuinely afford it.\n\nThe other grew up somewhere money was rarely discussed at all, simply available when needed. As an adult, this person finds spending easy and saving harder, not from carelessness, but because nothing in their early experience taught them saving mattered.\n\nNeither person is behaving irrationally. Both are behaving exactly as their early experience trained them to. The value of noticing this is not to judge either pattern as right or wrong. It is to recognise that a pattern exists at all, so it can become a choice rather than an unconscious default.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I do not have a money mindset. I just make rational decisions.\"**\n\nNo one's financial decisions are entirely free of an underlying mindset -- this belief is worth stating plainly rather than softening. What feels like plain rationality is usually a pattern so familiar it no longer feels like a pattern at all. The goal of this lesson is not to remove that influence, which is not possible, but to be able to name it, so that a decision can be examined rather than simply followed.", "riskUncertainty": "Noticing a pattern is not the same as changing it, and this lesson will not attempt to change anything on its own. Self-reflection has real limits. You might misidentify your own pattern, or resist naming one that feels uncomfortable to admit, and that is a normal and expected part of this kind of reflection, not a sign you are doing it wrong.\n\nIt is also worth being honest that a single lesson cannot undo years of an inherited pattern. What this lesson offers is a starting point for awareness, not a finished answer. Where a pattern feels significant or difficult to sit with, that is exactly the kind of thing worth raising with a coach rather than working through alone.", "reflection": "- What did money feel like in your household growing up: safe, tense, rarely discussed, something else entirely?\n- Where do you notice that same feeling showing up in how you handle money today?", "action": "Write down one belief about money you can trace back to how you were raised. You do not need to judge it as good or bad. Simply name it."}, {"id": "1.1.2", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Your Personal Financial Position", "keyTakeaway": "An honest rough picture beats a perfect one you keep avoiding.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to state your current income, essential spending, and savings position without needing to check.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1, Lesson: Money Mindset.", "concept": "A financial position is just three numbers, treated honestly: income, essential spending, and what is left over. It is not a complex financial analysis, and it does not require special tools, software, or training to work out.\n\nMost of the anxiety people feel here comes from avoidance rather than from any actual complexity in the numbers themselves. The figures are usually easy to find. What is hard is choosing to look at them without flinching, particularly if it has been a while since anyone did.\n\nOnce written down honestly, these three numbers give you something concrete to work with. Everything from the next lesson onward assumes you have this picture, even in rough form.", "context": "This lesson builds directly on Money Mindset. Having named a pattern in how you relate to money, this is where that awareness meets the actual facts of your position, moving from feelings about money to a clear, current picture of it.\n\nIf Money Mindset was about understanding why you feel the way you do about money, this lesson is about setting that feeling aside just long enough to look at the numbers plainly.", "example": "Consider one illustrative month, written down the way this exercise asks you to write down your own: honestly, and without waiting until it looks tidier. Take-home pay for the month comes to 2,400. Essential spending, rent, bills, food, transport, comes to 1,750. Discretionary spending, everything else that went out, comes to 450. What is left over is 200.\n\nNotice that this is not a budget, and it does not tell anyone what to do with the amount left over. It is simply a starting point: three numbers, written down truthfully, for one real month.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I need a perfect, detailed breakdown before this is useful.\"**\n\nAn honest rough picture is more valuable than a precise one you keep avoiding out of perfectionism. Three approximate but truthful numbers, written down today, will do more for you than a meticulously detailed spreadsheet you never quite get around to finishing.\n\nPrecision can wait. Honesty cannot -- the exercise fails the moment a number gets quietly rounded in a flattering direction.", "riskUncertainty": "A single month's snapshot can be misleading if that particular month's income or spending was unusually high or low: an unexpected bill, a bonus, a holiday. Treat this exercise as a useful starting point rather than a definitive, permanent picture.\n\nIt is worth repeating this exercise periodically as your circumstances change, rather than treating today's numbers as fixed. A financial position is a moving picture, not a single photograph.", "reflection": "- What surprised you, doing this exercise?\n- What is one number you have been avoiding looking at closely?", "action": "Write down your actual, not estimated, income, essential spending, and current savings for the most recent month you can check."}, {"id": "1.1.3", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Building Income, Spending, and Saving Systems", "keyTakeaway": "Systems beat willpower.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe at least one system, not just a one-off decision, that would make saving or investing happen automatically.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1, Lesson: Your Personal Financial Position.", "concept": "Systems beat willpower. There is a meaningful difference between deciding to save or invest, which relies on remembering and choosing every single time, and building a system where it happens without a decision being made at all. The second approach removes the exact moment where willpower tends to fail.\n\nThis is a long-standing principle in personal finance, sometimes called **pay yourself first**: treating your own saving or investing as the first thing that happens with your income, not the thing left over once everything else has been decided.\n\nOne useful way to think about it: treat the amount you save or invest the same way you treat rent or a utility bill. Nobody debates whether to pay the electricity bill each month, it is simply committed, non-negotiable, and paid on time. Saving and investing work the same way once they are set up as a system rather than a decision.\n\nThis is not a claim that willpower does not matter anywhere in personal finance. It is a narrower and more useful claim: for a repeated behaviour like saving or investing, willpower is an unreliable engine, because it has to win the same argument against itself every single month. A system only has to be built once.", "context": "This lesson moves from the static snapshot built in Your Personal Financial Position to an ongoing process. Where that lesson gave you a clear picture of a single month, this one is about making sure next month, and the month after, do not require you to redo that work from scratch.\n\nThis is where control over your financial foundation, the outcome this module is building toward, starts to become something real rather than a one-off exercise.", "example": "Consider what happens on payday under two different approaches. In the first, a transfer to savings or investments is set up to happen automatically the moment income arrives, before anything else is spent. The amount is decided once, in advance, and treated exactly like any other committed bill. In the second, nothing is set up in advance. Spending happens across the month as it comes up, and whatever remains at the end, if anything, is what gets set aside.\n\nBoth approaches can involve the same person with the same income. The difference is not effort or intention. It is ordering: whether saving or investing happens before spending has the chance to use up what is available, or only after.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I will save whatever is left at the end of the month.\"**\n\nThis approach reliably fails, because spending tends to expand to fill whatever is available. It is not usually a single large purchase that derails it, it is dozens of small, individually reasonable ones, each of which feels fine in isolation.\n\nSaving or investing first, before spending decisions are made, is the mechanism that actually works. Not because it requires stronger willpower, but because the money in question is no longer visible as available to spend in the first place, the same way a bill leaves your account before you ever get the chance to spend that money elsewhere.", "riskUncertainty": "Automating saving or investing before an emergency fund exists can backfire. If every spare pound is already committed to an automatic transfer, an unexpected cost has nowhere to come from except debt, which defeats the purpose of building the system in the first place.\n\nSequencing matters here, and that is exactly what the next lesson addresses directly. Building the system described in this lesson is still worthwhile, it simply needs a buffer sitting underneath it before it is pushed too far.", "reflection": "- What is one manual decision you make every month that could become automatic instead?", "action": "Identify one system change you could make this month. You do not need to execute it yet, naming it is the action for this lesson."}, {"id": "1.1.4", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Emergency Funds", "keyTakeaway": "An emergency fund protects your investments from being sold at the wrong moment.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why an emergency fund exists and state your own current emergency fund status.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1, Lesson: Building Income, Spending, and Saving Systems.", "concept": "An emergency fund exists to absorb financial shocks, so that an unexpected cost never forces you to sell investments or take on debt at a bad moment. A car repair, a period without income, an urgent home repair, none of these are truly unpredictable in the sense of being unforeseeable, only unpredictable in their exact timing.\n\nThis is not a separate topic from saving and investing. It is a precondition for it, which is exactly what this module's final lesson builds toward. Money that is saved or invested toward a longer-term goal needs time to work undisturbed. An emergency fund is what buys that time, by making sure a short-term shock never forces a decision about longer-term money.", "context": "This follows directly from the sequencing point raised in Building Income, Spending, and Saving Systems. Where that lesson flagged that automating saving or investing too early can backfire, this lesson explains precisely what it is protecting against, and why the order in which these systems are built genuinely matters.", "example": "Consider an unexpected cost arriving without warning: an appliance failing, an urgent repair, a month without the income you were counting on. The cost itself is not the difficult part. What matters is where the money to cover it comes from.\n\nWithout a buffer already in place, that cost has nowhere else to come from except debt, or money that was set aside for something else entirely, pulled out at a moment you did not choose.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I will just use a credit card if something comes up.\"**\n\nAn emergency fund is what prevents debt from becoming the default response to a shock. A credit card can cover an unexpected cost in the moment, but it converts a one-off problem into an ongoing one, with interest attached to it going forward.\n\nUsed occasionally and paid off quickly, a credit card is not inherently a problem. Used as the actual emergency fund, it quietly becomes one.", "riskUncertainty": "There is no single correct emergency fund size. It depends on factors specific to you, job security, dependants, health, and your own comfort with disruption, and this lesson deliberately avoids stating a fixed number of months as a universal rule, since that would edge toward personal advice rather than education.\n\nWhat is true for everyone is the direction, not the destination: some buffer is better than none, and a partial buffer is a real starting point, not a failure to have a complete one.", "reflection": "- If something unexpected cost you money next month, what would you actually do?", "action": "State your current emergency fund status honestly, full, partial, or none, as a starting point rather than a judgement on where you are."}, {"id": "1.1.5", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Financial Goals", "keyTakeaway": "A vague goal produces vague decisions.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to state at least one specific, personally meaningful financial goal.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1, Lesson: Emergency Funds.", "concept": "Goals give saving and investing a purpose beyond the abstract. \"Grow my money\" is not a goal in any actionable sense. \"Have a deposit for a home in three years\" is. The difference is specificity, and specificity is what allows a goal to actually inform a decision later.\n\nA specific goal answers questions a vague one cannot: how much, by when, and how that changes the way you think about risk and time horizon. None of those questions can be answered against \"grow my money,\" which is why vague goals tend to produce equally vague decisions further down the line.\n\nThis lesson is not asking you to commit to a life plan. It is asking for one goal, stated clearly enough that you could recognise the moment you achieved it.", "context": "This is where the module starts pointing forward. Everything up to this lesson, including Emergency Funds, has been about seeing your current position clearly and protecting it. This lesson is the first to ask where you actually want that position to go.\n\nThe goals named here are exactly what later saving and investing content, beginning in this module's final lesson and continuing well beyond it, will eventually need to serve. A goal without a horizon or a purpose is difficult to plan around, this lesson exists to give the rest of the platform something concrete to connect to.", "example": "Consider a few illustrative goal types, generic categories rather than personal financial planning. A short-term purchase: something specific within the next year or two. A medium-term milestone: a deposit, a wedding, a career change. Long-term security: retirement, financial independence. Each becomes more useful to you the more specific it gets.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"My goal is just to have more money.\"**\n\nThis is too vague to act on. More money could mean almost anything, over almost any timeframe, for almost any reason, which means it cannot actually guide a single decision.\n\nThe aim of this lesson is to push toward specificity, without prescribing what your particular goal should actually be. That choice, including how ambitious or modest it is, remains entirely yours.", "riskUncertainty": "Goals change, and that is entirely normal. A goal stated here is not a binding commitment, and nothing about this platform treats it as one. Circumstances shift, income changes, priorities change, life happens in ways no plan fully anticipates.\n\nRevisiting or replacing a goal later is expected, not a sign that something went wrong the first time. The value of stating one now is having something concrete to revise, rather than starting from nothing each time.", "reflection": "- What is one thing you are saving or investing toward, specifically?\n- How would you know if you had achieved it?", "action": "Write down one specific financial goal with a rough timeframe attached to it."}, {"id": "1.1.6", "moduleId": "1.1", "title": "Why Investing Matters", "keyTakeaway": "Saving preserves money; investing gives it room to grow.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain, in your own words, why investing, rather than only saving, matters for reaching longer-term goals.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1, Lesson: Financial Goals.", "concept": "Saving preserves money. Investing gives money the chance to grow faster than cash typically erodes in value over time. Both are legitimate tools, and this lesson is not an argument that one replaces the other, it is an argument that they do different jobs.\n\nCash sitting in an account is doing exactly what it is meant to do: staying stable and accessible. What it is not doing is growing in any meaningful sense, and over long enough periods, its real value tends to be gradually eroded by rising prices.\n\nThis lesson introduces that distinction conceptually. The actual mechanics of markets, risk, and compounding are deliberately deferred to Stage 2, where they can be given the full attention they need, rather than compressed into the closing minutes of this module.", "context": "This is the module's final lesson, and it functions as a bridge into Stage 2. Everything before this lesson has been about seeing your position clearly and building the systems to protect it and give it purpose. This lesson explains why that foundation is worth building toward something more.\n\nNothing here is asking you to act yet. It is simply naming the reason the rest of this platform exists: saving alone, however well organised, is unlikely to be enough for goals that sit many years away.", "example": "Consider the same starting amount, shown two ways: sitting in cash, or given room to grow through investing, over a long horizon. The purpose of comparing them is not to predict a real return. It is to show the shape of the difference between money that stays still and money that has time working in its favour.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Investing is basically gambling.\"**\n\nThis is addressed by distinguishing time horizon and purpose, not by asserting investing is safe, which it is not. Gambling has no reasonable basis for expecting a return over time, the odds are structurally against the person playing. Investing, approached with a long horizon and a clear purpose, does have a reasonable basis for expecting growth, even though any individual outcome remains uncertain.\n\nThat distinction, a reasonable basis versus no basis at all, is the actual difference, not the presence or absence of risk.", "riskUncertainty": "Investing involves real risk, including the possibility of loss. This lesson must not leave the impression that investing is simply better than saving in every circumstance. An emergency fund and short-term security, covered in the two lessons before this one, still matter regardless of how compelling investing's long-term case is.\n\nThe full risk conversation is coming in Stage 2, and this lesson says so explicitly, rather than implying the topic has already been covered here. Nothing in this lesson should be read as a signal that the hard part is behind you.", "reflection": "- What has stopped you from investing before now, if anything?", "action": "No specific action beyond continuing on to Investor Mindset. This lesson is a bridge, not a call to act on anything yet."}, {"id": "1.2.1", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Long-Term Thinking", "keyTakeaway": "Time is the engine, not the prediction.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why investing is a long-horizon activity rather than a short-term prediction game.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.1.", "concept": "Investing rewards people who commit capital for years, not people who correctly guess what happens next week. This is easy to say and harder to actually feel, because almost everything else in modern life rewards speed. News arrives instantly. Prices update by the second. It is natural to assume that reacting quickly to that information is what separates a good investor from an average one.\n\nIt is the opposite. Time, not speed, is the mechanism that makes investing work. A long enough horizon allows a genuinely good decision to play out, absorbs short-term noise that would otherwise look alarming, and gives compounding room to do its work. Shorten that horizon enough, and investing stops behaving like investing at all. It starts behaving like speculation: a series of short-term bets where being right quickly matters more than being right eventually.", "context": "You have just finished Financial Foundations, where you looked honestly at your financial starting point. Before this platform introduces a single investing concept, it wants you to understand the posture investing actually requires: patience over prediction. Everything from here forward, including the mechanics you will learn in Stage 2 and the frameworks introduced later in this platform, sits on top of this idea. Skipping past it to get to the more technical material would mean building on ground that has not been properly laid.", "example": "Picture two people who each put money into the same investment on the same day.\n\nThe first checks the price every morning, reacts to every dip, and moves their money elsewhere the moment it looks uncertain. The second checks in occasionally, stays invested through periods that look uncomfortable, and does not treat every short-term wobble as new information worth acting on.\n\nNeither person has any special insight the other lacks. The difference is not intelligence or access to better information. It is time horizon, and the discipline to actually hold it. Over a long enough period, that difference in behaviour tends to matter far more than any single decision either of them makes along the way.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Successful investors predict what happens next.\"**\n\nMost do not, and the ones who claim to consistently are worth treating with real scepticism. What successful, disciplined investing usually looks like is positioning for a wide range of possible outcomes over a long period, not calling the next move correctly. Prediction makes for a better story. Patience is what actually tends to work.", "riskUncertainty": "Long-term thinking is not a guarantee. It changes the odds in your favour over time, but it does not remove the possibility of a poor outcome for any one person, in any one period. Markets can remain difficult for longer than feels reasonable, and a long horizon does not promise comfort along the way, only a better chance of a good outcome by the end of it.\n\nIt is also worth being honest that \"long term\" is easy to commit to in principle and harder to hold to when an investment looks genuinely uncomfortable in practice. This lesson can explain the logic clearly. It cannot promise you will feel calm applying it when the moment actually arrives. What time horizon actually buys you is a better chance, not a guaranteed outcome, and the two are not the same thing even though they can feel similar when things are going well.", "reflection": "- What is the longest you have ever committed to something without a guaranteed result?\n- What helped you stay committed, or what made you give up early?", "action": "Write down one thing outside of money where patience paid off for you, even though it did not feel guaranteed at the time."}, {"id": "1.2.2", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Common Behavioural Mistakes", "keyTakeaway": "These patterns are normal, not personal failings.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to name at least two common behavioural mistakes investors make.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 1.2.1.", "concept": "Certain mistakes show up again and again in how people handle money, regardless of how intelligent or informed they are. Three of the most common: chasing recent strong performance, assuming it will continue; panic-selling during a downturn, out of a need to do something rather than nothing; and becoming overconfident after a run of good decisions, mistaking luck for skill.\n\nNone of these are failures of intelligence. They are consistent, well-documented patterns in how people generally behave under uncertainty, financial or otherwise. Naming them here is not about pointing out flaws. It is about giving you a way to recognise these patterns when they show up in your own decisions later in this platform, and in real life.", "context": "This lesson builds directly on Long-Term Thinking. Knowing that patience matters is one thing. Recognising the specific behaviours that pull you away from it is what actually makes patience possible to practise in a real moment, rather than only in principle. The three patterns below are the ones that show up most often across investors of every experience level, and they are worth learning to spot early, before they have already shaped a decision you cannot easily undo.", "example": "Consider someone who sees an investment perform unusually well over a short period and decides to put more money into it, assuming the trend will continue. Then consider someone else who sees an investment fall in value and sells immediately, uncomfortable with holding something that looks like it is losing.\n\nBoth people are responding to the same underlying instinct: recent movement feels like it must mean something important about the future. In both cases, the decision is driven by what just happened rather than by any change in the underlying reasoning for holding the investment in the first place. That gap, between reacting to recent movement and reasoning through an actual decision, is where most common investing mistakes live.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"This only happens to unsophisticated investors.\"**\n\nIt does not. These patterns show up in experienced, knowledgeable investors as often as in beginners. Expertise in the mechanics of investing does not automatically protect against the emotional pull of recent events. This is worth taking seriously rather than assuming it applies to someone else and not to you.", "riskUncertainty": "Being able to name a bias in the abstract does not mean you will catch it in yourself in the moment it actually happens. Awareness helps, but it is not a cure, and this lesson will not pretend otherwise. You are likely to make some version of these mistakes at some point, and that is a normal part of being human, not a sign this lesson failed to teach you something.\n\nIt is worth adding that these three patterns are not the only ones that exist, only the most common and easiest to recognise once named. Real behaviour under uncertainty is often messier than any tidy list can capture, and a decision can be driven by more than one of these patterns at once, which can make it genuinely difficult to untangle in the moment, even with the best intentions.", "reflection": "- Which of these three patterns, chasing performance, panic-selling, or overconfidence, do you recognise most in yourself?\n- Has this pattern shown up somewhere outside of money too?", "action": "Think of one past financial decision, however small, and identify whether one of these three patterns was quietly driving it."}, {"id": "1.2.3", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Fear and Greed", "keyTakeaway": "Fear and greed are the two forces most likely to move you off your plan.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe how fear and greed distort investing decisions at emotional extremes.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 1.2.2.", "concept": "Fear and greed sit at opposite ends of the same problem: both push people toward decisions driven by emotion rather than reasoning. Greed shows up as the pull to chase something that looks like it is working, often later than is sensible. Fear shows up as the pull to escape something that looks like it is failing, often at exactly the point it would have been better to stay put.\n\nThese two forces are not occasional visitors. They are a constant, quiet pressure sitting underneath many financial decisions, and they tend to be strongest at precisely the moments when calm reasoning matters most.", "context": "Where Common Behavioural Mistakes named specific behavioural mistakes, this lesson looks at the two underlying emotional forces driving many of them. Understanding fear and greed as forces, rather than isolated bad decisions, makes them easier to notice as they are happening rather than only in hindsight, once the decision has already been made and cannot be easily undone. This distinction, between noticing a feeling in the moment and only recognising it afterward, is the whole point of learning to name these two forces deliberately.", "example": "Imagine a period where an investment has been rising steadily for some time. Greed, in this context, does not look dramatic. It looks like a quiet, growing sense that missing out would be worse than any risk of getting in late.\n\nNow imagine the same investment falling sharply. Fear, here, does not look dramatic either. It looks like a quiet, growing sense that doing something, anything, would feel better than sitting still and tolerating the discomfort.\n\nIn both cases, the emotional pull feels like practical thinking in the moment. It rarely announces itself as fear or greed. That is precisely what makes it worth learning to recognise deliberately, rather than trusting it will be obvious when it happens.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I would recognise it in myself in the moment.\"**\n\nThis is, in practice, one of the hardest biases to catch live. It is far easier to identify fear or greed in someone else's decision, or in your own decision after the fact, than to notice it clearly while you are in the middle of feeling it.", "riskUncertainty": "This lesson deliberately avoids describing any current market mood or condition, because doing so would risk sounding like commentary on what is happening right now rather than a general pattern worth understanding at any time. Fear and greed are described here as forces that exist across all market conditions, not a description of anything currently taking place.\n\nIt is also worth naming plainly that understanding this concept intellectually is not the same as being immune to it. Even people who teach this exact lesson are not exempt from feeling it themselves, and there is no version of experience or expertise that fully removes the pull of these two forces. The most that awareness realistically buys you is a slightly better chance of noticing the feeling before it becomes a decision, not a guarantee that you will.", "reflection": "- Think of a non-investing decision where fear or excitement pushed you somewhere you later regretted. What did that feel like while it was happening?", "action": "No written action required for this lesson. Carry the question above with you into Patience, the next lesson."}, {"id": "1.2.4", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Patience", "keyTakeaway": "Patience is a system, not a personality trait.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why patience functions as a practised skill rather than a fixed personality trait.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 1.2.3.", "concept": "It is tempting to assume some people are simply naturally patient investors and others are not, as though it were a fixed trait like eye colour. In practice, patience in investing is closer to a skill built through systems and habits than a personality someone either has or lacks.\n\nPeople who appear patient with money have usually built structures that make patience easier to maintain: automated saving so a decision does not need remaking every month, clear reasons for holding an investment written down in advance, or simply less frequent checking of prices. Patience, examined closely, often turns out to be design rather than temperament.", "context": "This lesson answers a question the previous three lessons raise naturally: if fear, greed, and common behavioural mistakes are this powerful, what actually helps someone resist them? Patience, built deliberately rather than hoped for, is a large part of the answer, and it is the last piece needed before this module turns to compounding, where the practical payoff of that patience actually becomes visible in concrete, countable terms. Without this piece in place first, compounding can look like an abstract mathematical curiosity rather than something that actually rewards how you choose to behave.", "example": "Consider someone who reviews their investments once a year, on a set date, rather than whenever a headline catches their attention. This is not because they lack interest or discipline. It is because they have deliberately reduced the number of moments where fear or greed gets an opportunity to influence a decision.\n\nCompare that to someone with no such structure, checking prices daily, and reacting to any documented downward movement. Both people may care equally about their financial future. One has simply built a system that makes patience the default, rather than something they have to consciously will themselves into every day.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Patient investors just do not care as much.\"**\n\nThe opposite is often closer to true. Building the kind of system described above usually requires more upfront thought and intention than simply reacting to whatever happens day to day. Patience, done well, is often more deliberate and engaged, not less.", "riskUncertainty": "Patience is not the same as blind persistence regardless of new information. This lesson is not suggesting you should ignore a genuinely changed situation simply because changing course feels uncomfortable. The distinction between staying patient through normal uncertainty and ignoring a real, material change is a real one, and it is not always obvious in the moment which situation you are actually in.\n\nThis is a genuine limitation of the concept, not a small caveat. A system built to encourage patience can, in rare cases, also delay a response that was actually warranted. There is no simple rule that resolves this tension perfectly, which is part of why this kind of judgement call is exactly the sort of thing worth discussing with a coach rather than deciding alone in the moment.", "reflection": "- What helps you stay patient in other areas of your life, outside of money?\n- Could something similar apply to how you handle financial decisions?", "action": "Identify one small structural change, such as a fixed review date, that could reduce how often you are tempted to react to short-term movement."}, {"id": "1.2.5", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Compounding", "keyTakeaway": "Compounding rewards time more than timing.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain compounding in your own words and why time matters more than most people initially expect.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 1.2.1.", "concept": "Compounding means growth building on top of previous growth, rather than growth calculated fresh each time from the same starting point. The effect is small and unremarkable at first, and becomes dramatic only with enough time behind it. This is precisely why compounding is easy to underestimate: its early stages look nearly identical to doing nothing at all.\n\nThe practical consequence is that time matters more than most people initially expect, often more than the specific amount invested at the start. A modest amount invested for a long period can end up outperforming a larger amount invested for a shorter one, purely because of how much longer compounding has had to work.", "context": "Having covered patience as a practised skill in Patience, this lesson explains one of the clearest reasons that patience actually pays off mechanically, not just psychologically. Compounding is the concrete, structural reward for the long-term thinking introduced back in Long-Term Thinking, and understanding it properly is what makes the discipline asked for in the earlier lessons of this module feel genuinely worth the sustained effort, rather than simply worth believing in on faith alone. It is the closest thing this module offers to visible proof that the earlier lessons were pointing somewhere real.", "example": "Imagine two people investing the same amount each month. One starts ten years earlier than the other, then stops adding new money entirely, letting the existing amount simply sit and grow. The other starts later but keeps contributing steadily for longer.\n\nDespite contributing far less in total, the person who started earliest often ends up with more, purely because their money has had more time for growth to build on previous growth. This is not a trick or a rare exception. It is simply what compounding does, consistently, given enough time.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Compounding guarantees wealth.\"**\n\nIt does not. Compounding works only when the underlying capital survives and remains invested. It describes how growth can build on itself over time. It does not remove the real possibility of loss, and treating it as a guarantee is one of the more damaging misunderstandings a new investor can carry forward.", "riskUncertainty": "Compounding assumes continued growth over time, but it says nothing about the path that growth actually takes. Real investing involves periods of decline, sometimes lengthy ones, along the way. Compounding describes the shape of a general long-term outcome. It does not promise a smooth journey to get there, and a downturn along the way does not mean compounding has stopped working, only that the path is not a straight line.\n\nIt is worth being direct about the scale of this limitation. The examples used to illustrate compounding, including the one above, are simplified on purpose, and real outcomes will not follow such a clean pattern. Compounding is a genuine, powerful mechanic, but it is not a formula that produces a predictable number, and treating it as one is a common and understandable mistake worth guarding against here, early, before it hardens into an assumption.", "reflection": "- What is one area of your life, outside of money, where small consistent effort has compounded into a much larger result over time?", "action": "No written action required. Carry forward the distinction between compounding as a mechanism and compounding as a guarantee into the next lesson."}, {"id": "1.2.6", "moduleId": "1.2", "title": "Probabilistic Thinking", "keyTakeaway": "Investing deals in likelihoods, not certainties.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe investing outcomes as a range of probabilities rather than a single predicted result.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 1.2.1.", "concept": "Investing decisions are best understood in terms of likelihoods, not certainties. Rather than asking what will definitely happen, a more useful question is what range of outcomes is reasonably possible, and how likely each one actually is. This shift, from a single predicted answer to a range of weighted possibilities, is a fundamentally different way of thinking than most people apply by default.\n\nThis idea will return in more depth later in this platform, particularly once you reach decision-making frameworks in Stage 3. For now, the goal is simply to start noticing when you are treating an uncertain outcome as though it were a single, known answer.", "context": "This lesson closes Investor Mindset by tying together everything covered so far, long-term thinking, common mistakes, fear and greed, and patience, into one underlying mindset: comfort with genuine uncertainty, rather than a need to resolve it prematurely into false certainty. This same idea will return in more depth later in this platform, particularly once you reach decision-making frameworks in Stage 3, so it is worth genuinely absorbing here rather than simply skimming past it on the way to something else.", "example": "Imagine being asked whether a particular investment will be worth more in five years. A prediction-based answer forces a single response: yes or no. A probabilistic answer looks different. It might sound like: there is a reasonable chance of meaningful growth, a smaller but real chance of a poor outcome, and a wide range of plausible results in between, depending on conditions that cannot be known in advance.\n\nThe second answer is less satisfying to hear. It is also considerably more honest, and closer to how experienced, disciplined investors actually think, even when they do not say it out loud in those exact terms.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Experts should be able to predict this precisely.\"**\n\nEven highly experienced, well-informed investors deal in probabilities, not certainties. The expectation that genuine expertise should produce precise predictions is a misunderstanding of what expertise in this field actually offers: better-reasoned probabilities, not certain answers.", "riskUncertainty": "Probabilistic thinking can be misused just as easily as prediction can. It is possible to construct a plausible-sounding range of probabilities after an outcome is already known, and convince yourself it was obvious in advance. This kind of hindsight reasoning feels like probabilistic thinking but is actually closer to storytelling. Genuine probabilistic thinking is done honestly before an outcome is known, not reconstructed afterward to fit it.\n\nThere is also a subtler risk worth naming. Thinking in probabilities can start to feel like a shield against ever being wrong, since almost any outcome can be described as having been \"possible\" in advance. Used honestly, this sharpens your reasoning. Used carelessly, it can become a way of avoiding real accountability for a decision.", "reflection": "- Where else in your life do you already think in probabilities rather than certainties, even if you have never described it that way?", "action": "Write down one financial decision you are currently facing, or expect to face soon, and describe it in terms of a range of possible outcomes rather than a single predicted result."}, {"id": "2.1.1", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "What Investing Is", "keyTakeaway": "Investing is deploying capital with a reasonable basis for return, not a bet.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to distinguish investing from speculation and explain what separates the two.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 1.2.", "concept": "Investing means putting capital into something with a reasonable, examinable basis for expecting a return over time. That basis might be a business generating profit, a government or company repaying a loan with interest, or an asset with a genuine claim on future income. The key word is reasonable: there is a case that can be explained and examined, even if the outcome is never guaranteed.\n\nSpeculation looks similar on the surface but lacks that basis. It is a bet on price movement itself, disconnected from any underlying reason to expect a return. This distinction is not about which one feels riskier. Both carry real risk. It is about whether there is something examinable underneath the decision, or only a hope that the price moves in your favour.", "context": "You have completed Stage 1's work on your own financial foundation and mindset. This is the first lesson of Stage 2, where the platform introduces the actual mechanics of investing. Getting this distinction right at the very start shapes how clearly you will be able to evaluate everything that follows in this stage, since every vehicle covered from here forward, shares, bonds, funds, can be approached either as genuine investing or as disguised speculation, depending entirely on the reasoning behind the decision.", "example": "Imagine two people each putting money toward a company. The first has looked at what the company does, how it earns money, and believes it will likely continue operating profitably. The second has no view on any of that, and is putting money in purely because the price has been rising and they expect it to keep rising.\n\nBoth people might end up with the same result. But only the first person has an examinable reason for the decision, one that could be discussed, challenged, and revisited if circumstances changed. The second person's reasoning, if it can be called that, is simply a bet on momentum continuing.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Investing and gambling are basically the same thing.\"**\n\nThis deserves to be taken seriously rather than dismissed. Both involve real uncertainty and the possibility of loss. The genuine difference is the presence of an examinable basis for the decision. Investing without doing that examination starts to blur into speculation, regardless of what you call it.", "riskUncertainty": "Having an examinable basis for a decision does not make investing safe. Businesses fail. Loans go unpaid. Even a well-reasoned investment can lose value, sometimes for reasons that had nothing to do with the original reasoning at all. This distinction between investing and speculation is about the quality of the decision-making process, not a guarantee about the outcome, and it is worth being direct that a sound process can still produce a disappointing result.\n\nIt is also worth being honest that this lesson cannot tell you how to fully evaluate whether a given decision has a genuinely sound basis. That skill is built gradually across this platform, particularly in Stage 3, where real frameworks for evaluating businesses and evidence are introduced properly. For now, the goal is simply recognising that the distinction exists and matters, not yet being able to apply it with confidence to a real decision.", "reflection": "- Think of a financial decision you have made in the past. Did it have an examinable basis, or was it closer to a bet on price movement?", "action": "No written action required. Carry this distinction with you into the next lesson, where it applies directly to a specific type of investment."}, {"id": "2.1.2", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "Stocks", "keyTakeaway": "A share is a claim on a real business, not just a price.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what owning a share of stock actually represents.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.1.1.", "concept": "A share of stock is a small piece of ownership in a real, operating business. Owning one gives you a genuine, if usually very small, claim on that business: on its profits, its decisions through voting rights, and its future. This is easy to forget once a share simply becomes a number that moves on a screen, but the ownership underneath it is real, not symbolic.\n\nThis matters because it reframes what you are actually doing when you buy a share. You are not purchasing a lottery ticket tied to a price. You are purchasing a small stake in an underlying business, with all the genuine uncertainty that ownership of any business carries.", "context": "This lesson applies the distinction from What Investing Is, between investing and speculation, to the most commonly discussed investment vehicle. Understanding a share as ownership, rather than as a price, is the foundation the rest of Stage 2 and much of Stage 3 will build on directly, particularly once this platform introduces how investors actually assess whether a business is worth owning. Getting this foundational idea right now will make several later, more technical lessons considerably easier to follow, since they all assume this ownership framing as a starting point rather than re-explaining it each time.", "example": "Imagine buying a single share in a company that makes and sells furniture. You now own a tiny fraction of that company: its factories, its brand, its future profits or losses. If the company sells more furniture and becomes more profitable over time, your small stake is, in principle, worth more, because the business itself is worth more.\n\nThe share's price on any given day is one thing. The underlying business you actually own a piece of is another. They are related, but they are not always the same, and that gap between price and underlying value is worth holding in mind from this point forward.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"A stock price is the value of the company.\"**\n\nPrice and value can diverge, sometimes significantly, and this idea will be developed properly in Stage 5. For now, it is enough to hold the two ideas as genuinely separate: the price is what the market is currently willing to pay, and the value is a separate question about what the underlying business is actually worth.", "riskUncertainty": "Share prices can move for reasons that have very little to do with the underlying business's actual health, at least in the short term. A share can fall in price without anything real changing about the company behind it, and it can rise without anything real improving either. This gap between price movement and business reality is a genuine source of risk, not just a curiosity, because it can make a sound business look troubled, or a troubled one look sound, at least temporarily.\n\nThis lesson deliberately does not attempt to explain how to judge whether a company is a good business. That is real, substantial work covered properly later in this platform, and attempting it here would be premature.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, did you think of a share mainly as a price, an ownership stake, or something else entirely?", "action": "No written action required. This lesson sets up the ownership concept the rest of Stage 2 depends on."}, {"id": "2.1.3", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "Bonds", "keyTakeaway": "A bond is a loan, not ownership.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what a bond represents, as distinct from a share.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.1.2.", "concept": "Where a share represents ownership, a bond represents lending. When you buy a bond, you are lending money to whoever issued it, a government or a company, in exchange for a promise: regular interest payments, and the return of your original amount at an agreed future date. You do not own any part of the issuer. You are simply owed money by them.\n\nThis distinction, lending versus owning, is one of the most fundamental in investing, and it shapes almost everything else about how bonds behave differently from shares, including how they respond to risk and to changes in the wider economy.", "context": "Having established what ownership means through shares in the previous lesson, this lesson introduces the other major category most new investors encounter: lending. Understanding the difference clearly now prevents a common and persistent confusion later in this platform, particularly once funds that combine both categories are introduced in the next lesson. Many people use the word \"investment\" loosely to cover both, without noticing that lending and owning carry genuinely different risks and behave differently under different conditions.", "example": "Imagine lending a friend a sum of money, with an agreement that they will pay you a small amount of interest each year and return the full amount after five years. A bond works on the same basic principle, just formalised and issued by a government or company rather than a friend.\n\nIf that friend's financial situation changes for the worse, your ability to actually get repaid changes too, even though the original agreement has not changed on paper. The same is true of a bond: the promise behind it is only as strong as the issuer's actual ability to keep it.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Bonds are risk-free.\"**\n\nBonds are often described as safer than shares, and in some respects they are, but risk-free is not accurate. Bonds carry credit risk, the chance the issuer cannot repay, and interest-rate risk, where a bond's value can fall if interest rates rise after it was issued. Both are genuine, examinable risks, not theoretical ones.", "riskUncertainty": "The interest-rate risk mentioned above surprises many new investors, because it feels counterintuitive at first. If new bonds start offering higher interest payments than an existing bond does, that existing bond becomes less attractive by comparison, and its market value tends to fall, even though nothing about the original promise has changed. This is a real, structural feature of how bonds work, not an edge case.\n\nCredit risk is the more intuitive of the two, but it is still easy to underestimate. Not every issuer is equally likely to repay what they owe, and a bond's stated interest rate often reflects that risk directly: higher promised interest can be a signal of higher underlying risk, not simply a better deal.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, would you have assumed bonds carried no real risk? What changed after reading this?", "action": "No written action required. Hold the lending-versus-owning distinction in mind for the next lesson."}, {"id": "2.1.4", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "Funds and ETFs", "keyTakeaway": "A fund pools many holdings into one investment.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what a fund or ETF is and the purpose it serves.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.1.2, Lesson 2.1.3.", "concept": "A fund, or an exchange-traded fund, is not a separate asset class alongside shares and bonds. It is a container that pools many individual holdings, shares, bonds, or both, into a single investment you can buy as one unit. The purpose is straightforward: rather than buying dozens of individual shares yourself, a fund lets you gain exposure to many at once, through a single purchase.\n\nThis mechanism exists primarily to make diversification practical. Building a genuinely diversified collection of individual holdings yourself would take significant time, research, and money. A fund does that pooling for you, in a single, more manageable product.", "context": "Having covered shares and bonds individually, this lesson introduces the vehicle most people actually use to hold a mix of both in practice. Understanding funds as containers, not a separate category of asset, prevents a common source of confusion later in this platform, especially once the platform starts discussing portfolio construction directly. Many people encounter funds before they properly understand shares or bonds individually, which is precisely why this lesson is placed after both, rather than before them.", "example": "Imagine wanting exposure to a hundred different companies, rather than picking just one or two yourself. Buying each of the hundred shares individually would be slow, costly, and difficult to manage well over time. A single fund built to hold all hundred lets you achieve a similar outcome through one purchase instead.\n\nThe fund itself is not doing anything mysterious. It is simply holding the underlying shares on your behalf, in agreed proportions, and giving you an indirect claim on all of them at once through the units you own in the fund, rather than owning each individual share yourself.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"A fund guarantees diversification is done.\"**\n\nThis depends entirely on what the fund actually holds. A fund built around a hundred companies across many different industries offers genuine diversification. A fund built around ten companies in a single narrow sector does not, regardless of the word \"fund\" appearing in its name. The label does not do the diversifying. The actual composition does.", "riskUncertainty": "Not every fund is diversified in a meaningful way, and assuming otherwise is a genuine, common mistake. A narrow, sector-specific fund can be just as concentrated, and carry similar risk, as owning a small handful of individual shares directly, even though it technically contains many holdings. The word \"fund\" describes a structure, not a guarantee about risk.\n\nIt is also worth noting plainly that pooling holdings together does not remove the underlying risk of what is being held. A fund built entirely around businesses in genuine financial distress carries that distress with it, spread across many holdings rather than concentrated in one, but not eliminated.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, did you assume any fund was automatically well diversified? What would you check now before assuming that again?", "action": "No written action required. This distinction, between the fund structure and what it actually holds, matters throughout the rest of this platform."}, {"id": "2.1.5", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "Index vs. Active", "keyTakeaway": "Tracking a market and trying to beat it are two different goals.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe the basic distinction between index and active investing approaches.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.1.4.", "concept": "An index approach aims to simply track a defined part of the market, rising and falling roughly in line with it, rather than trying to outperform it. An active approach involves deliberately selecting holdings, or adjusting them over time, with the explicit goal of doing better than a straightforward market average.\n\nBoth are coherent, defensible approaches, built on different assumptions about markets and what is achievable within them. This lesson introduces the basic distinction only. Which approach might suit a given investor, and the arguments each side makes, is a genuinely deeper question, deliberately addressed later, once you have more of the platform's frameworks available to reason it through properly.", "context": "Having covered the main vehicles, shares, bonds, and funds, this lesson introduces one of the fundamental philosophical splits in how those vehicles get combined and managed. It is intentionally left unresolved here, since resolving it prematurely would get ahead of frameworks you have not yet built, and Understanding Investment Approaches, later in this platform, exists specifically to give it the fuller treatment it deserves. This lesson's job is simply to make sure you can recognise the distinction and describe it accurately, not to help you decide anything about it yet.", "example": "Imagine two funds, both giving you exposure to a similar group of companies. One is built to simply mirror that group as closely as possible, moving in line with it automatically without anyone actively choosing what to hold. The other is actively managed by someone deciding which of those companies to hold more or less of, based on their own judgement, with the explicit aim of doing better than the first fund over time.\n\nNeither approach is inherently right or wrong. They represent genuinely different assumptions about markets, and reasonable, informed people disagree about which is more likely to work well over time.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Active managers always try harder and therefore do better.\"**\n\nEffort and outcome are not the same thing here. Whether active management tends to outperform index approaches over time is a genuinely contested, well-studied question, not a settled one, and this lesson deliberately does not resolve it. Trying harder does not automatically translate into a better result.", "riskUncertainty": "This lesson must stop short of comparing the two approaches directly or suggesting which one is likely to suit you personally. That comparison, and the reasoning behind it, is explicitly the task of Understanding Investment Approaches, once you have more tools available to evaluate it properly. Attempting it here, with only this lesson's introductory framing behind you, would risk a shallow conclusion presented as a settled answer.\n\nIt is worth being honest that this deferral might feel unsatisfying if you were hoping for a direct answer here. That is a deliberate choice, not an oversight: a real comparison deserves the fuller treatment Understanding Investment Approaches gives it, not a rushed verdict at this early stage.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, did you already have an assumption about which approach was better? Where do you think that assumption came from?", "action": "No written action required. This question is picked up properly in Understanding Investment Approaches."}, {"id": "2.1.6", "moduleId": "2.1", "title": "Asset Ownership", "keyTakeaway": "Every vehicle here is a different route to ownership or lending.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what it actually means to own each type of asset covered in this module.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.1.2, Lesson 2.1.3, Lesson 2.1.4, Lesson 2.1.5.", "concept": "This module has covered four vehicles: shares, bonds, funds, and the index-versus-active distinction that applies across them. Underneath all four sits one core distinction worth holding onto clearly: ownership versus lending. A share is ownership. A bond is lending. A fund is a container that can hold either, or both, in combination. None of these are interchangeable, even though they are often discussed loosely as though they were, particularly in casual conversation or marketing material that flattens real structural differences into vague, similar-sounding language.\n\nRecognising which category a given investment actually falls into is a genuinely useful habit, one this platform will keep returning to as more complexity gets introduced from Stage 3 onward.", "context": "This lesson closes Investing Fundamentals by tying its four vehicles together into a single, coherent picture, rather than leaving them as four separate, disconnected facts. Getting this synthesis right now makes the more applied material in Stage 3 considerably easier to follow, since much of that later material assumes you can already tell, quickly, whether you are looking at ownership or lending underneath any given investment, without needing to stop and work it out from scratch each time.", "example": "Imagine looking at a mixed collection of investments and asking, for each one, a simple question: am I lending money here, or do I own a piece of something real? A share and a fund built mostly of shares answer that question one way. A bond and a fund built mostly of bonds answer it the other way.\n\nThis single question, asked consistently, cuts through a surprising amount of confusion that builds up when these vehicles are described only by their names, ISAs, funds, ETFs, without reference to what is actually happening underneath each one, and it is a habit worth carrying forward well beyond this module.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"All these vehicles are basically interchangeable.\"**\n\nThey are not. Each carries a genuinely different ownership structure and risk profile, even where the vehicles are sometimes marketed or discussed as though they were broadly similar. Understanding the actual structure underneath a given investment matters more than the label attached to it.", "riskUncertainty": "This module has deliberately stayed at the level of definitions and mechanics. It has not attempted to tell you which of these vehicles is right for your own situation, and it is worth being explicit that this module cannot do that safely. Vehicle choice depends on personal circumstances this platform does not have access to, and applying these concepts to your actual situation is exactly the kind of question worth bringing to a coaching conversation rather than deciding from this lesson alone.\n\nIt is also worth naming that this module has only covered the most commonly encountered vehicles, not an exhaustive list. Real investing involves other structures beyond these four, and this module's simplicity here is a deliberate starting point, not a claim that nothing else exists.", "reflection": "- Of the four vehicles covered in this module, which one do you feel least confident explaining to someone else right now?", "action": "Write a short explanation, in your own words, of the difference between owning and lending, as if explaining it to someone with no financial background at all."}, {"id": "2.2.1", "moduleId": "2.2", "title": "What a Wrapper Means", "keyTakeaway": "A wrapper is the container, not the investment.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what a tax wrapper is, conceptually, without reference to any specific figures.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 2.1.", "concept": "A tax wrapper is the account, or container, that an investment sits inside. It is not the investment itself. The same underlying share or fund can sit inside different types of wrapper, and the wrapper is what determines how any growth or income from that investment gets treated for tax purposes. Changing the wrapper does not change what you actually own. It changes the tax rules that apply to owning it, which is a genuinely important distinction even though the two are often discussed as if they were the same thing.\n\nThis distinction, container versus contents, is the single idea this lesson exists to establish clearly, before any specific wrapper types are introduced in the next lesson.", "context": "You have just finished Module 2.1's introduction to the main investment vehicles: shares, bonds, and funds. This lesson introduces a separate, related idea that sits alongside those vehicles rather than replacing them. Deliberately, this lesson and the two that follow it contain no specific figures, thresholds, or dates. Those change regularly, and belong in the Client Hub, which is built to stay current. This module explains what wrappers are and why they exist, not what currently applies.", "example": "Imagine the same investment, say, a fund holding a broad range of companies, held in two different accounts: one designed for tax-advantaged saving, and one with no special tax treatment attached at all. The underlying fund is identical in both cases, held by the same person, with the same holdings inside it. What differs is entirely how any growth is treated when it comes to tax, nothing about the investment itself.\n\nThis is easy to lose sight of once wrapper names become familiar, because people often talk about \"investing in an ISA\" as though the ISA itself were the investment. It is not. It is the container the investment happens to be held inside.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"The wrapper is the investment.\"**\n\nThis is the exact misunderstanding this lesson exists to correct. A wrapper has no growth or return of its own. It is a tax treatment applied to whatever is actually held inside it, and confusing the two makes every later lesson on wrappers harder to follow clearly.", "riskUncertainty": "Because this module deliberately avoids specific figures, thresholds, and dates, it cannot tell you what actually applies to your situation right now. That information lives in the Client Hub, which is built to be kept current, rather than in a lesson that would otherwise need frequent updating to remain accurate. Treat this module as background understanding, not as a source of current, actionable figures.\n\nIt is also worth noting that tax treatment is a genuinely complex area, and this module, by design, only covers the conceptual layer. Real decisions about wrappers depend on your personal circumstances in ways this lesson cannot address, which is exactly the kind of question worth bringing to a coaching conversation once you have the conceptual grounding this module provides.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, did you think of a wrapper as a type of investment, or as something separate from the investment itself?", "action": "No written action required. Carry the container-versus-contents distinction into the next lesson, where specific wrapper types are introduced."}, {"id": "2.2.2", "moduleId": "2.2", "title": "General Purpose of Each Wrapper Type", "keyTakeaway": "Different wrappers exist for different purposes, not interchangeable labels.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe the general category and purpose of an ISA, a SIPP or pension, and a General Investment Account, without reference to specific figures.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.2.1.", "concept": "Three main wrapper categories exist in the UK, and each serves a genuinely different purpose. A Stocks and Shares ISA is a form of tax-advantaged saving, generally intended for medium to longer-term goals, with growth typically sheltered from certain taxes. A SIPP, or pension, is built specifically around long-term retirement saving, with its own distinct set of tax treatments and rules around when the money can actually be accessed. A General Investment Account carries no special tax treatment at all, and is typically used once other wrapper allowances have been used, or where the flexibility of no restrictions matters more than the tax treatment.", "context": "This is the module's central lesson, and the one most tempted toward adding specific figures, since wrapper categories are usually discussed alongside their current allowances. This lesson deliberately resists that pull. The goal here is category and purpose only: what each wrapper is broadly for, not what currently applies to any of them, which the Client Hub covers properly and keeps current. Holding that discipline here matters more than in almost any other lesson in this module, since it is the one most likely to drift toward stating a figure if a writer is not careful.", "example": "Imagine three people with different goals. One is saving toward a house deposit in around five years, and values flexibility alongside some tax advantage. Another is saving specifically for retirement decades away, and is comfortable with money being inaccessible until then in exchange for its own distinct tax treatment. A third has already used the allowances available to them elsewhere, and simply wants somewhere flexible to hold further investments without restriction.\n\nEach of these three people is likely to end up considering a different wrapper category, not because one wrapper is objectively better, but because each wrapper is built around a genuinely different purpose.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"All wrappers work the same way, just with different names.\"**\n\nThey do not. Each of the three categories described above carries a distinct purpose, a distinct set of tax treatments, and in the case of pensions, genuinely different rules about when the money becomes accessible at all. Treating them as interchangeable, differing only in name, leads to real, avoidable confusion.", "riskUncertainty": "This is the lesson in this module where a writer, or a reader, is most likely to want a specific figure: an allowance, a threshold, an age. None appear here, deliberately. Those figures change, sometimes annually, and presenting them here would mean this lesson could become inaccurate without anyone noticing. The Client Hub exists specifically to carry that current information, with tools built for exactly this purpose.\n\nIt is worth being direct that understanding these three categories at a conceptual level is not the same as knowing which one, or which combination, suits your own circumstances. That is a genuinely personal question, shaped by your goals, timeline, and existing arrangements, and it belongs in a coaching conversation, not in this lesson.", "reflection": "- Which of the three wrapper categories described here sounds most relevant to a goal you currently have, even without knowing the specific figures involved yet?", "action": "No written action required. The next lesson addresses how wrapper choice relates to investment choice."}, {"id": "2.2.3", "moduleId": "2.2", "title": "Wrapper Choice vs. Investment Choice", "keyTakeaway": "Choosing where to hold an investment is separate from choosing what to hold.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why choosing a wrapper is a separate decision from choosing what to invest in.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.2.2.", "concept": "The same underlying investment, a particular fund, for instance, can sit inside an ISA, a SIPP, or a General Investment Account. The investment itself does not change depending on which wrapper holds it. What changes is the tax treatment applied to any growth or income it produces. These are genuinely two separate decisions: what to invest in, and where to hold it, even though they are often discussed, and made, as though they were one single choice, particularly when a provider presents both choices side by side on the same screen at the same time, which encourages them to blur together in practice rather than being treated as distinct.", "context": "Having covered the general purpose of each wrapper type in the previous lesson, this lesson addresses a subtler point that often gets missed: wrapper and investment are independent decisions that happen to get made at the same time, not one combined decision. Keeping them mentally separate makes both decisions clearer, and prevents a poor investment choice from being excused simply because the wrapper around it happened to be the right one, or a good investment choice from being wasted inside a wrapper that does not suit it.", "example": "Imagine choosing to invest in a particular fund. That decision, on its own, says nothing yet about which wrapper it should sit inside. The fund could sit in an ISA, a SIPP, or a General Investment Account, and remain exactly the same fund, with the same holdings and the same underlying risk, in every case, regardless of which wrapper eventually holds it.\n\nNow imagine choosing a wrapper first: deciding an ISA suits your goals, before deciding what to actually hold inside it. That, too, is a separate decision, made independently of which specific fund or share eventually goes inside it. Neither decision determines the other, even though they will ultimately be made together in practice.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"I need to pick my wrapper and my investments as one decision.\"**\n\nMany people experience wrapper and investment choice as a single, combined decision, largely because platforms and providers often present them together. Separating the two mentally, even while making both decisions around the same time, tends to produce clearer reasoning for each one individually.", "riskUncertainty": "Recognising that these are two separate decisions does not tell you how to actually make either one for your own situation. This lesson stays at the level of the distinction itself, not personal application, because personal application depends on your specific goals, timeline, and existing wrapper usage, none of which this platform has visibility into.\n\nIt is also worth noting that in practice, the two decisions are not fully independent in every circumstance. Certain wrappers may restrict what can be held inside them, which means wrapper choice can, at times, genuinely constrain investment choice. This lesson keeps things simple deliberately, and that simplification is worth being aware of rather than assumed away entirely.", "reflection": "- Have you previously experienced choosing a wrapper and an investment as one single decision? What would it look like to separate them?", "action": "No written action required. The final lesson in this module directs you to where current wrapper information actually lives."}, {"id": "2.2.4", "moduleId": "2.2", "title": "Where to Find Current Rules", "keyTakeaway": "Current figures live in the Hub, not in this lesson.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will know where to find current wrapper allowances, thresholds, and tools, without this lesson attempting to state any of them directly.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.2.3.", "concept": "Everything specific about wrappers, current allowances, eligibility rules, exact tax treatment, lives in the Client Hub, not in this education platform. The Hub is built and maintained specifically to stay current, including interactive tools designed to help you explore how different wrappers might apply to your own numbers. This lesson's entire purpose is the hand-off itself: pointing you toward where genuinely current information lives, now that you understand the underlying concepts this module has covered, rather than leaving you with concepts alone and nowhere practical to take them next, which would make everything covered so far considerably less useful in practice.", "context": "This closes Module 2.2 by putting the module's design constraint into practice directly. Rather than this lesson apologising for what it cannot tell you, it treats the Hub hand-off as a genuine, deliberate feature of how this platform and the Hub work together, each doing the job it is actually built for, so that neither one tries to do the other's job less well than it could, and so that neither ever needs to apologise for what the other is better placed to handle.", "example": "Picture finishing this module with a clear sense of what an ISA, a SIPP, and a General Investment Account are each generally for, but without any current figures attached to any of them. That is exactly the intended outcome. The next step is the Client Hub, where the current allowances for each wrapper type are kept accurate and up to date, alongside tools built specifically to help you explore what those figures might mean for your own situation, in a way this static lesson never could.\n\nThis is not a gap in this module. It is the module working as designed: durable understanding here, current specifics there.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"This lesson should just tell me the current allowance.\"**\n\nIt is a reasonable thing to want, but it is deliberately not this lesson's job. A number stated here would need constant updating to stay accurate, and would eventually become wrong without anyone noticing, exactly the failure this module's design is built to avoid. The Hub exists precisely so this platform does not have to carry that burden.", "riskUncertainty": "This lesson's entire content is a signpost, not a claim about any actual figure, so the main risk here is different from other lessons: the risk is stopping at this lesson without actually following through to the Hub. Understanding wrapper concepts without ever checking current figures leaves you with only half of what you need to make an actual decision.\n\nIt is also worth noting plainly that even the Hub's current information is not personal advice. It can tell you what currently applies in general. It cannot tell you which wrapper, or combination of wrappers, is right for your specific situation. That remains a personal question, best explored with a coach once you have both the concepts from this module and the current figures from the Hub in hand.", "reflection": "- What is one wrapper-related question you now feel ready to look into on the Client Hub?", "action": "Visit the Client Hub and locate the current information for at least one wrapper type covered in this module."}, {"id": "2.3.1", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "What Is a Market?", "keyTakeaway": "A market is a mechanism, not a mystery.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to define a market as a mechanism connecting buyers and sellers, rather than a mysterious or unpredictable force.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 2.1.", "concept": "A market, at its core, is simply a mechanism that brings buyers and sellers together and allows them to agree on a price. It is not a mysterious force with intentions of its own, and it does not \"decide\" anything in the way that phrase sometimes implies. It is a structure, built to let people with different views on value exchange with each other, over and over, continuously.\n\nThis might sound almost too simple to be worth stating, but it matters, because much of the language used to describe markets, markets \"reacted,\" markets \"punished,\" markets \"feared,\" implies intention and emotion that a mechanism does not actually have. Understanding a market as a mechanism, not an actor, is the foundation this entire module builds on.", "context": "You have completed the coverage of wrappers in UK Investment Wrappers. This module shifts focus to a different, related question: how the markets that sit underneath any investment, in any wrapper, actually work. This first lesson deliberately stays purely definitional, with no judgement about current conditions, before building toward more specific mechanics across the rest of this module. Everything from liquidity to price movement to expectations, covered in the lessons that follow, assumes this basic mechanism as its starting point, which is why it is worth establishing clearly and patiently here first.", "example": "Picture a physical marketplace, with many stalls, buyers walking between them, and prices shifting slightly as goods sell out or sit unsold. A financial market works on a similar underlying logic, just conducted at far greater speed and scale, and without the physical stalls. Buyers and sellers, each with their own view on what something is worth, meet continuously, and prices emerge from that ongoing interaction.\n\nNo single person or institution sets the price directly. It emerges from many individual decisions happening at once, which is precisely what makes a market a mechanism rather than a single decision-maker with a view of its own.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Markets are unpredictable by nature.\"**\n\nThis framing treats markets as though they have a hidden, chaotic will of their own. A more accurate way to think about it: markets aggregate an enormous amount of information and opinion continuously, and the result of that aggregation can be difficult to predict, which is a different claim than the market itself being inherently random or wilful.", "riskUncertainty": "Describing a market as a mechanism does not make it simple or fully understood, even by people who study it closely. The aggregation of so many individual decisions happening simultaneously produces genuine complexity, and this lesson's simplified framing should not be mistaken for a complete explanation of how markets actually behave in practice, however useful it is as a corrective to the more dramatic, intention-filled language markets are often described with.\n\nIt is also worth noting that this definitional framing, useful as a starting point, does not yet explain why prices move the way they do, which is covered properly later in this module. Treat this lesson as the foundation the rest of the module builds on, not as the full picture on its own, and be wary of assuming that removing intention from the description also removes the genuine complexity underneath it.", "reflection": "- Before this lesson, did you think of \"the market\" as something with intentions or moods of its own?", "action": "No written action required. Carry the mechanism framing forward into the next lesson, which looks more closely at how buyers and sellers actually interact."}, {"id": "2.3.2", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "Buyers, Sellers, and Liquidity", "keyTakeaway": "Liquidity is about ease of exit, not stability.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain what liquidity means and why it matters for how easily an asset can be bought or sold.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.3.1.", "concept": "Liquidity describes how easily something can be converted to cash without needing to significantly change its price to find a buyer or seller. A highly liquid asset has many buyers and sellers active at once, meaning a trade can usually happen quickly, close to the last agreed price. A less liquid asset has fewer active participants, meaning finding a buyer or seller may take longer, or may require accepting a less favourable price to make the trade happen at all.\n\nThis is a genuinely separate idea from how much an asset's price moves, which is covered properly later in this module, and conflating the two is one of the more common sources of confusion for new investors.", "context": "Having established markets as a mechanism in the previous lesson, this lesson looks more closely at one of the practical features that mechanism produces: how easily participants can actually enter or exit. Liquidity affects nearly every other concept covered later in this platform, including funds, portfolios, and risk, which is why it is introduced early and referenced repeatedly rather than treated as a single, standalone topic covered once and set aside, never to be mentioned again.", "example": "Imagine two assets. One trades constantly, with a large number of buyers and sellers active throughout each day, meaning a trade can typically happen almost immediately, close to the current agreed price. The other trades rarely, with long gaps between transactions, meaning finding a willing buyer or seller might take considerably longer, and might require accepting a noticeably different price than the last one recorded.\n\nBoth assets could, in principle, be equally stable or unstable in terms of how much their price moves over time. Liquidity describes something different: how easily you could actually get in or out at a given moment, regardless of how much the price itself has been moving.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Liquidity and volatility are the same thing.\"**\n\nThey describe genuinely different features of an asset. Volatility describes how much a price moves. Liquidity describes how easily a trade can actually happen. An asset can be highly liquid and still volatile, or illiquid and relatively stable, and treating the two as interchangeable causes real confusion later when both concepts are discussed together.", "riskUncertainty": "Illiquid assets carry a genuine, easy-to-overlook risk: they can appear stable simply because they trade infrequently, not because their underlying value is genuinely steady. Without frequent trades to reveal a current price, an illiquid asset's apparent stability can be somewhat illusory, only becoming clear when an actual attempt is made to buy or sell it, sometimes at a considerably less favourable price than expected.\n\nThis is worth taking seriously rather than treating as a minor technical detail. In periods of genuine market stress, liquidity can also change quickly across many assets at once, which is a real, structural risk worth understanding conceptually now, even though this lesson does not go further into what causes those broader shifts, since that belongs to a more advanced discussion than this introductory module attempts.", "reflection": "- Can you think of something outside of investing, a house, a specialist item, where liquidity, in this sense, clearly affects how easily it could be sold?", "action": "No written action required. Liquidity will come up again as this platform discusses funds and portfolios later in Stage 2 and beyond."}, {"id": "2.3.3", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "Price Discovery", "keyTakeaway": "Price is a constantly negotiated estimate, not a hidden truth.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain how prices form through the interaction of buyers and sellers, rather than being fixed or revealed from somewhere else.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.3.2.", "concept": "Price discovery is the ongoing process by which a market arrives at a current price, through the continuous interaction of buyers and sellers, each acting on their own view of value. It is not a single event that reveals some hidden, objectively correct number. It is a constant, moving negotiation, happening simultaneously across enormous numbers of participants, each contributing their own view through the act of buying or selling.\n\nThis framing matters because it reframes what a price actually represents: not a fixed truth waiting to be uncovered, but an ongoing, continuously updated estimate, shaped by whoever happens to be participating at any given moment.", "context": "Building directly on the previous two lessons, this lesson explains the actual mechanism by which the buyers and sellers already introduced arrive at an agreed price. Understanding price discovery as a process, not an event, sets up the next lesson's discussion of why prices actually move over time, and it is the piece most people skip past without realising how much it shapes everything that follows, including how to interpret a single day's movement later on.", "example": "Imagine an auction with no fixed price, where bidders continuously raise and lower their offers based on new information, competing bids, and their own shifting sense of value. The final price at any given moment reflects the current balance of everyone's views, not a hidden, correct number being slowly revealed to whoever waits long enough to see it.\n\nFinancial markets work in a broadly similar way, just continuously, across enormous numbers of participants at once, rather than in a single room with a small group of bidders. The price you see at any moment is simply where that ongoing negotiation currently sits, not a finished conclusion anyone has actually reached.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"There is a true price that the market eventually finds.\"**\n\nThis framing assumes a hidden, correct number exists and is gradually being revealed. A more accurate way to think about it: the current price is a constantly negotiated estimate, shaped by whoever is participating right now, not a fixed truth being slowly uncovered over time.", "riskUncertainty": "Price discovery can be wrong, in the sense of later turning out not to reflect an asset's underlying reality, for extended periods of time. Markets are not infallible simply because they aggregate many participants' views continuously. A price can sit meaningfully apart from what later analysis suggests the underlying business or asset was actually worth, sometimes for a considerable stretch of time before that gap narrows.\n\nThis is a genuine limitation worth taking seriously, not a rare edge case. It is part of why this platform treats price and underlying value as two related but distinct ideas throughout, a distinction that becomes considerably more important once Stage 5 covers valuation directly.", "reflection": "- Does thinking about price as an ongoing negotiation, rather than a fixed number waiting to be revealed, change how you think about a price you have seen move recently?", "action": "No written action required. This idea, that price discovery can be wrong for a while, sets up the next lesson directly."}, {"id": "2.3.4", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "Why Prices Move", "keyTakeaway": "Prices respond to expectations as much as facts.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe the main, general drivers of price movement without reference to any specific current event.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.3.3.", "concept": "Prices move as a result of new information changing participants' views of value, shifts in how many buyers or sellers are active at a given time, or changes in expectations about the future. These drivers interact constantly, and no single one operates in isolation from the others. A price is, at any moment, the combined result of everything currently shaping how participants view an asset's worth.\n\nThis lesson deliberately stays entirely generic, describing the categories of driver that exist rather than any specific instance of them, since discussing a specific current driver would risk sounding like commentary on present conditions rather than a lasting, general explanation.", "context": "Having covered how prices form through discovery in the previous lesson, this lesson turns to why that discovered price keeps changing over time. Understanding the general categories of driver here is what makes the next two lessons, on expectations and on short-term versus long-term value, possible to follow clearly, since both build directly on the categories introduced in this lesson rather than introducing entirely new mechanics of their own that would need to be learned separately.", "example": "Imagine a piece of new information becoming available about a business: something changes about its prospects, positively or negatively. Participants update their individual views of value in response, and the price shifts as those updated views interact through the market. No single participant needs to act alone for this to happen. It emerges from the combined effect of many people updating their views around the same time.\n\nEqually, a shift in how many buyers or sellers are active, even without new information about the underlying business itself, can move a price, simply by changing the balance between the two sides of the market.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Prices move because of news alone.\"**\n\nNews is one driver among several, not the only one. Shifting expectations, changes in how many participants are actively buying or selling, and evolving views on value all contribute, often together, and reducing price movement to news alone misses much of what is actually happening underneath any given price change.", "riskUncertainty": "This is the lesson in this module most tempted toward discussing a specific, current event as an illustration, and it deliberately avoids doing so. Using a live example here would risk this lesson reading as commentary on present market conditions, rather than a lasting explanation of general mechanics that remains accurate regardless of when it is read.\n\nIt is also worth being honest that identifying, in hindsight, exactly which driver caused a given price movement is often genuinely difficult, even for people who study markets closely. Multiple drivers frequently overlap, and disentangling their individual contributions after the fact is rarely as clean as this lesson's simplified categories might suggest.", "reflection": "- Of the drivers described here, which one do you think you have previously overweighted or underweighted in how you think about price movement?", "action": "No written action required. The next lesson looks specifically at expectations, one of the drivers introduced here."}, {"id": "2.3.5", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "The Role of Expectations", "keyTakeaway": "Markets react to surprises, not just news.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why markets often react to whether something met, beat, or missed expectations, rather than to the underlying fact alone.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.3.4.", "concept": "Markets frequently respond less to a raw fact itself, and more to whether that fact matched, exceeded, or fell short of what participants were already expecting. A genuinely positive piece of news can still see a price fall, if it was less positive than expected. A genuinely disappointing piece of news can see a price hold steady, or even rise, if it was less bad than participants had already priced in.\n\nThis happens because prices, per the previous two lessons, already reflect participants' current expectations before any new information arrives. New information moves a price to the extent it changes those expectations, not simply because of its own standalone content.", "context": "This lesson deepens Why Prices Move's point about expectations as a driver, since it is often the least intuitive of the drivers covered and deserves closer attention on its own. Understanding this properly is what makes the final lesson in this module, on short-term price versus long-term value, genuinely land rather than feel abstract, since expectations are the mechanism that makes short-term price movement so often disconnected from what has actually, objectively changed about the underlying business or asset.", "example": "Imagine a business widely expected to perform poorly over a coming period. If it then performs only moderately poorly, meaningfully better than the low expectation already built into its price, the price can rise, even though the actual result was still, by any objective measure, a disappointing one for the business itself.\n\nThis can feel counterintuitive at first. The explanation is that the price had already adjusted downward in anticipation of a worse outcome, so a result better than that already-lowered expectation represents genuinely new, relatively positive information, even though the underlying result itself was still weak in absolute terms.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Good news should always push a price up.\"**\n\nWhether news is good in an absolute sense and whether it is good relative to what was already expected are two different questions, and markets tend to respond more to the second than the first. This is one of the more genuinely counterintuitive ideas in this module, and worth sitting with rather than dismissing quickly.", "riskUncertainty": "Because expectations are not directly observable, only inferred from prices and behaviour, it is genuinely difficult to know with confidence what a market was actually expecting before a given piece of news arrived. This makes explanations built around expectations somewhat harder to verify than explanations built around a single, observable fact, and worth holding with appropriate humility.\n\nIt is also worth noting that this framing, useful as it is, can be used loosely to explain almost any price movement after the fact, in a way that risks sounding more precise and confident than it actually is. A genuinely honest use of this idea acknowledges its limits rather than treating it as a complete explanation for everything.", "reflection": "- Has a price move ever surprised you because the underlying news seemed, on its own, to point the other way?", "action": "No written action required. This idea sets up the module's final lesson directly."}, {"id": "2.3.6", "moduleId": "2.3", "title": "Why Short-Term Price Does Not Reflect Long-Term Value", "keyTakeaway": "Reacting to short-term price swings usually costs more than it protects.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why reacting to short-term price movement often leads to poor decisions, and why short-term price does not always represent long-term value.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.3.5.", "concept": "Everything covered so far in this module, price discovery, the various drivers of price movement, and the role of expectations, combines to produce a price that reflects the current, collective view of many participants at this moment, not a settled judgement about an asset's genuine long-term worth. Short-term price and long-term value are related, but they are not the same thing, and treating short-term movement as a reliable signal about long-term worth is a common, understandable, and genuinely costly mistake.\n\nThis is the direct, practical payoff of everything this module has covered: understanding why reacting to short-term price movement, in either direction, often leads to decisions that do not hold up well once more time has passed.", "context": "This lesson closes How Markets Work by bringing together price discovery, the drivers of movement, and the role of expectations into one practical conclusion. It also directly answers the module's own revised Before-to-After outcome, and is worth reading as the payoff for everything the previous five lessons have carefully built toward, rather than as a separate, unrelated topic tacked onto the end of the module as an afterthought. Everything before this point has been building the vocabulary and reasoning this final, practical conclusion actually depends on.", "example": "Imagine an investor who sees a short-term price fall and interprets it as clear evidence that something is fundamentally wrong with the underlying business, prompting them to sell. Now imagine another investor who understands that short-term price reflects a constantly shifting, collective view, not a settled judgement, and who checks whether anything has genuinely changed about the underlying business before reacting to the price movement at all.\n\nThe first investor may be right occasionally, but is reacting to the price itself, not to genuine evidence. The second investor is applying everything this module has covered to make a more grounded, considered decision, even though it takes more discipline to do so.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"If the price moved, something real must have changed.\"**\n\nSometimes something real has genuinely changed. Often, per this module, it reflects shifting expectations, participation, or sentiment rather than a fundamental shift in the underlying business or asset itself. Assuming every price movement reflects real, lasting change is a costly habit this module exists specifically to help you avoid.", "riskUncertainty": "This lesson should not be read as claiming that short-term price movement never carries genuine information. Sometimes it does, and dismissing every price movement as pure noise would be its own, equally serious mistake in the opposite direction. The genuine skill this module is building toward is judgement: learning to ask whether a given movement reflects real, lasting change or something more temporary, rather than assuming automatically in either direction without pausing to consider which it actually is.\n\nIt is also worth being honest that this judgement is genuinely difficult in the moment, even for experienced investors, and this lesson alone does not fully equip you to make that call reliably. That capability builds gradually across the rest of this platform, particularly in Stage 3 and Stage 4, where the tools for actually making this kind of judgement get introduced properly.", "reflection": "- Think of a time you reacted to a short-term price movement. Looking back, do you think something real had genuinely changed, or were you responding mainly to the movement itself?", "action": "Write down one question you could ask yourself in future, before reacting to a short-term price movement, to help judge whether something real has actually changed."}, {"id": "2.4.1", "moduleId": "2.4", "title": "What a Business Actually Is", "keyTakeaway": "A share is a claim on a real business, not a symbol on a screen.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to describe a business as a real, operating entity, and explain what a share actually represents a claim on.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 2.1.", "concept": "It is easy to talk about \"buying a stock\" and never once picture what that stock actually is. A ticker on a screen is not the thing itself. It is a label attached to something far more concrete.\n\nBehind every ticker is a real business. People show up to work. Products get made or services get delivered. Customers pay for something. Suppliers get paid. Rent gets paid. Wages get paid. All of this happens whether or not anyone is watching the share price that day.\n\nWhen you own a share, you own a small slice of that real activity. Not a symbol. Not a number that moves up and down. A fractional claim on an actual operating business, with real employees, real customers, and real decisions being made by real people.\n\nThis grounding matters more than it might first appear. Almost everything you will learn later in this platform, about analysing businesses, understanding value, and thinking like an investor, only makes sense once this foundation is in place. Skip it, and the rest becomes abstract. Keep it in view, and the rest becomes concrete.", "context": "This lesson sits right after Investing Fundamentals, where you learned the basic mechanics of what a share is. This lesson takes that mechanical definition and makes it real. It is the first lesson in the curriculum that asks you to picture an actual business rather than a financial instrument.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"A business is just its stock ticker.\"** The ticker is shorthand, nothing more. It exists so the business can be found and traded easily. It is not the business itself, any more than a house's postcode is the house. Confusing the two is one of the easiest ways to start thinking about investing as a game of symbols rather than a claim on something real.", "riskUncertainty": "None yet. This lesson is foundational and descriptive. It does not ask you to judge or evaluate anything, so there is nothing here that could be mistaken for a recommendation or a prediction.", "reflection": "Think of a business you interact with regularly, a shop, a service, an app you use. Picture the people behind it: who works there, what they actually do day to day, who their customers are. Notice how different that feels from picturing a ticker symbol.", "action": "Write down the name of one real business you interact with often, and list three things that actually happen inside it on a normal day."}, {"id": "2.4.2", "moduleId": "2.4", "title": "How Businesses Create Value", "keyTakeaway": "Value creation is a sustained pattern, not a single transaction.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain, in general terms, how a business generates value for the people who own it.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.4.1.", "concept": "At its simplest, a business creates value by selling something for more than it costs to produce and deliver, and doing this in a way that can continue over time.\n\nThat last part matters as much as the first. A business that sells at a profit once is not the same as a business that can keep doing so, year after year, while covering its costs, reinvesting in itself, and adapting as conditions change. Value creation is not a single transaction. It is a sustained pattern.\n\nThis is a genuinely simplified picture. Real businesses operate inside competition, changing customer needs, cost pressures, and countless factors that this one lesson cannot fully capture. The purpose here is not to give you a complete theory of business value. It is to give you a correct starting point that later lessons and later modules will build on.", "context": "This lesson builds directly on the grounding in what a business is from What a Business Actually Is, and moves from describing a business to describing how it actually creates value for its owners. It sets up the distinction you will need in the next lesson between revenue, profit, and cash.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Growth alone means value is being created.\"** A business can grow its sales while losing money on every one of them, or grow in a way that cannot be sustained. Growth without profitability, and profitability without durability, are not the same thing as genuine value creation. This distinction will come up again and again as you go further into the curriculum.", "riskUncertainty": "This lesson is deliberately simplified. Real value creation involves far more nuance than an introductory lesson can responsibly cover, including competitive pressure, changing customer behaviour, and the difficulty of sustaining an advantage over time. Treat this as a correct foundation, not a complete picture. Later modules build directly on this and add the missing depth.", "reflection": "Think of a business you believe creates real value. What is it actually selling for more than it costs to deliver, and what makes you think it can keep doing that over time, rather than just this year?", "action": "Write one sentence describing how your chosen business creates value, without using the word \"growth\" anywhere in it."}, {"id": "2.4.3", "moduleId": "2.4", "title": "Revenue, Profit and Cash: An Introduction", "keyTakeaway": "Revenue, profit, and cash are three different numbers, not one.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to distinguish revenue, profit, and cash generation at a basic level, and explain why they are not the same thing.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.4.2.", "concept": "These three words get used almost interchangeably in everyday conversation, but they mean genuinely different things, and the difference matters.\n\n**Revenue** is the total amount a business brings in from selling its products or services, before anything is taken out for costs.\n\n**Profit** is what remains after the business pays its costs, its wages, its suppliers, and everything else it takes to operate. A business can have enormous revenue and still make very little profit, or none at all, if its costs are high enough.\n\n**Cash** is the actual money moving in and out of the business day to day. A business can be profitable on paper and still run short of cash, if, for example, customers are slow to pay or the business has to spend heavily upfront before it sees a return.\n\nThis lesson introduces these three ideas only at a basic level. A full, working understanding of how to read and interpret them properly is the entire purpose of Reading Financial Information, later in this platform. For now, the goal is simply to stop treating these three words as if they mean the same thing.", "context": "This distinction is one you will lean on repeatedly from here forward. Reading Financial Information builds its entire content on the assumption that you already know revenue, profit and cash are different things, so this lesson exists specifically to make sure that foundation is in place before you get there.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Revenue and profit are basically the same thing.\"** They are not, and the gap between them can be enormous. A business can report headline-grabbing revenue figures while barely breaking even, or losing money outright. Learning to ask \"yes, but what was the profit\" is a genuinely useful habit to build early.", "riskUncertainty": "Nothing new here beyond what has already been said. This lesson is introductory by design, and deliberately does not go further into how these figures are actually read from a real financial statement. That depth comes later, in Reading Financial Information, once you have more foundation to build on.", "reflection": "Without looking anything up, try to explain in your own words the difference between revenue and profit to someone who has never heard either term before.", "action": "Write short, one-sentence definitions of revenue, profit, and cash, in your own words, without copying the definitions above."}, {"id": "2.4.4", "moduleId": "2.4", "title": "Why Business Quality Will Matter Later", "keyTakeaway": "Business quality is a real skill, previewed here and taught properly later.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to state why \"business quality\" becomes an important idea later in this platform, without yet analysing what makes a business good or bad.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.4.3.", "concept": "You now understand that a business is a real, operating thing, that it creates value by selling for more than it costs to deliver, and that revenue, profit and cash are three different measures of how it is doing. A natural question follows from all of this: how do you tell whether a business is actually good at what it does?\n\nThis lesson does not answer that question. It previews it.\n\nSome businesses sustain what they do far better than others. Some have advantages that are hard for competitors to copy. Some are run by people who make consistently sound decisions with the money the business generates. Learning to recognise these traits is a real skill, and it deserves real space, which is exactly what How Investors Think About Businesses and, later, Business Analysis at Depth are for.\n\nNaming this now, without teaching it yet, is deliberate. It gives you a signpost for where this thread of thinking is heading, so that when How Investors Think About Businesses arrives, it builds on something you already expect rather than something that appears out of nowhere.", "context": "This is the closing lesson of Businesses Create Value, and it functions as a bridge. It does not introduce new mechanics. It sets an honest expectation for what comes later, in Stage 3 and again, at greater depth, in Stage 5.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"This lesson should teach me how to judge quality now.\"** It deliberately does not. That is the job of How Investors Think About Businesses, and later, Business Analysis at Depth, done properly, with the space a genuine framework requires. Trying to shortcut that here would mean doing it badly.", "riskUncertainty": "None. This lesson makes a promise about what is coming, not a claim about any business or any method of judging one. There is nothing here that could be mistaken for guidance.", "reflection": "Without trying to build a framework, what is one thing that makes you personally trust that a business is well run, based only on your own experience as a customer or observer?", "action": "None beyond the reflection above. This lesson closes Businesses Create Value; the next new concept begins in Risk, Time and Diversification."}, {"id": "2.5.1", "moduleId": "2.5", "title": "Risk vs. Volatility", "keyTakeaway": "Volatility is movement. Risk is the chance of permanent loss.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain the difference between risk and volatility, and why treating them as the same thing leads to poor decisions.", "prerequisitesNote": "Module 2.1.", "concept": "In everyday conversation, \"risky\" usually means \"the price moves around a lot.\" That is not what risk actually means to an investor, and confusing the two is one of the most common and costly mistakes a beginner can make.\n\n**Volatility** describes how much a price moves, up and down, over a given period. Some investments move a great deal in short spaces of time. Others barely move at all. Volatility is observable. You can see it on a chart.\n\n**Risk**, more precisely, describes the chance of a permanent, lasting loss, of genuinely losing money you cannot get back, rather than simply watching a value move up and down along the way. An investment can be highly volatile and still be low risk in this sense, if the movement is temporary and the underlying value holds up over time. Equally, an investment can appear calm and steady while carrying real, hidden risk of permanent loss.\n\nThis distinction matters because volatility, on its own, is not something to fear. It is often simply the price of being invested in something that can grow over time. Risk, in the sense of permanent loss, is what genuinely deserves your attention.", "context": "This is the opening lesson of Risk, Time and Diversification, and it resets a piece of everyday language that gets used carelessly almost everywhere else. Everything else in this module, time horizons, diversification, and how uncertainty is actually managed, depends on this distinction being clear first.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"A big price swing means something has gone wrong.\"** A price swing, on its own, tells you very little. What matters is whether the swing reflects a genuine, lasting change in the underlying value, or simply short-term movement that says nothing about the long-term picture. How Markets Work covers this kind of short-term noise in more depth; this lesson simply establishes the vocabulary you need to reason about it correctly.", "riskUncertainty": "This distinction, while genuinely useful, is also a simplification. In practice, it is not always obvious in the moment whether a price movement reflects temporary noise or a real, lasting change in value. Even experienced investors get this judgement wrong. This lesson gives you the correct frame to reason with, not a formula that removes the uncertainty entirely.", "reflection": "Think of a time you saw an investment, or something you owned, drop sharply in value. Looking back, was that a genuine, lasting loss, or was it volatility that later passed?", "action": "Write down, in one sentence each, the difference between volatility and risk, in your own words, without copying either definition above."}, {"id": "2.5.2", "moduleId": "2.5", "title": "Time Horizons", "keyTakeaway": "A longer time horizon changes what risk means in practice.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why time horizon changes how risk should be thought about, and identify your own approximate time horizon for a given goal.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.5.1.", "concept": "Time horizon simply means how long you intend to hold an investment before you are likely to need the money. It sounds like a small detail. It is, in fact, one of the most important factors in how risk should be thought about at all.\n\nMoney you need in the next year behaves very differently, in terms of what matters, from money you will not touch for twenty years. A short time horizon leaves little room for volatility to smooth itself out. If a value drops sharply and you need the money soon after, that drop is far more likely to become a genuine, lasting loss for you personally, simply because you do not have time to wait it out.\n\nA longer time horizon changes the picture considerably. Short-term volatility that looks alarming in the moment has historically had much more room to smooth out over long stretches of time, though this is a general historical pattern, not a guarantee about any specific future period.\n\nThis is why the same investment can be entirely reasonable for one person and poorly matched to another, not because the investment itself is different, but because their time horizons are.", "context": "This lesson builds directly on Risk vs. Volatility's distinction between risk and volatility. Time horizon is one of the main reasons that distinction matters in practice: it changes how much a given amount of volatility should actually concern you.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"A long time horizon means risk does not matter.\"** It does not remove risk. It changes how much room there is to absorb volatility along the way. A long time horizon is not a guarantee of a good outcome. It is simply more time for a reasonable plan to play out.", "riskUncertainty": "Past patterns of volatility smoothing out over long periods are exactly that: past patterns. They are not a promise about any specific future period, and no time horizon, however long, removes the possibility of loss. This lesson describes a genuine, well-observed tendency, not a certainty.", "reflection": "Think about a specific financial goal you have, whenever it might be. Roughly how many years away is it? Does that feel like a short, medium, or long time horizon to you, and why?", "action": "Write down one financial goal and your honest, approximate time horizon for it, in years."}, {"id": "2.5.3", "moduleId": "2.5", "title": "An Introduction to Diversification", "keyTakeaway": "Diversification manages risk -- it does not remove it.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain, at a basic level, what diversification is and what it can and cannot do.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.5.2.", "concept": "Diversification means spreading money across a range of different things, rather than concentrating it all in one place. The reasoning behind it is straightforward: if one holding performs badly, it does not sink the entire picture, because other holdings are not necessarily affected in the same way at the same time.\n\nIt is worth being precise about what diversification actually does. It does not remove risk altogether, and it does not guarantee a good outcome. What it does is reduce the damage that any single bad outcome can do to the whole. This is a meaningful and genuinely useful thing, but it is a different claim from \"diversification makes investing safe,\" which overstates what it actually achieves.\n\nThis lesson introduces the idea only at this basic level. It does not attempt to teach you how to build a diversified portfolio yourself; that is a separate, practical skill that sits outside this module's scope.", "context": "This lesson connects directly to what you have just learned about risk and time horizon. Diversification is one of the main tools investors use specifically to manage the kind of risk described in Risk vs. Volatility, spreading exposure so that no single outcome can cause a permanent, catastrophic loss on its own.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"Diversification means I cannot lose money.\"** It reduces the impact of any single holding going badly wrong. It does not remove risk, and a well-diversified position can still lose value, particularly if many different things are affected by the same broad event at the same time.", "riskUncertainty": "Diversification is a genuinely useful tool, but it has real limits. It works best when the things being held do not all move for exactly the same reasons at exactly the same time, and in practice, that independence is never guaranteed, especially during periods of broad market stress. This lesson introduces the concept honestly, without overstating what it can deliver.", "reflection": "Think of a time you spread risk in some area of your life that was not money, choosing multiple options rather than relying on just one. What made you do that, and did it work the way you expected?", "action": "In your own words, write one sentence explaining what diversification does, and one sentence explaining what it does not do."}, {"id": "2.5.4", "moduleId": "2.5", "title": "Why Uncertainty Cannot Be Removed, Only Managed", "keyTakeaway": "No amount of sophistication removes uncertainty -- only manages it.", "objective": "By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain why uncertainty is a permanent feature of investing, and describe the difference between managing it and trying to eliminate it.", "prerequisitesNote": "Lesson 2.5.3.", "concept": "By this point in the module, you have learned three tools for thinking about risk: the distinction between risk and volatility, the effect of time horizon, and the basics of diversification. It would be natural to conclude that, used together, these tools remove uncertainty. They do not.\n\nNo amount of careful thinking, no amount of time, and no amount of spreading money across different things can guarantee a particular outcome. Uncertainty is not a flaw in the process that better tools eventually fix. It is a permanent, structural feature of investing itself, because the future genuinely cannot be known in advance.\n\nWhat the tools from this module actually do is change your relationship with that uncertainty. They do not remove it. They help you reason about it more clearly, avoid unnecessary and avoidable risks, and make more considered decisions in the presence of something that will never be fully resolved. That is a meaningfully different, and more honest, goal than eliminating risk altogether.", "context": "This is the closing lesson of Risk, Time and Diversification, and it deliberately pulls back from the specific tools covered in the previous three lessons to name the honest limit that sits underneath all of them. This same theme, that a framework can help without ever making an outcome certain, is one you will meet again later in the curriculum, including in Portfolio Principles.", "commonMisconceptions": "**\"If I do everything right, I should not lose money.\"** Doing everything sensibly reduces the chance and the scale of avoidable losses. It does not remove the possibility of loss altogether. Believing otherwise sets an expectation that reality cannot meet, and tends to produce real disappointment or poor decisions later when uncertainty inevitably shows up.", "riskUncertainty": "This entire lesson is, in effect, about risk and uncertainty directly, so there is nothing further to add beyond what has already been said. The honest position is this: nothing in this module, or anywhere else in this platform, can promise a specific outcome. What it can do is help you reason well in the presence of an uncertainty that never fully goes away.", "reflection": "Think of a decision you made carefully, with good reasoning, that still did not turn out the way you hoped. Looking back, was the reasoning actually wrong, or did an outcome you could not have known in advance simply happen anyway?", "action": "None beyond the reflection above. This lesson closes Risk, Time and Diversification; the next new concept begins in Understanding Investment Approaches."}],
  MODULES: [{"id": "0.0", "stage": 0, "stageName": "Alignment", "name": "Investor Learning Commitment", "type": "commitment", "purpose": "A short reflection confirming readiness to begin, not a pass or fail gate.", "prerequisites": [], "beforeAfter": null, "estimatedMinutes": 5, "coachingConnection": "None", "completionEvidence": "Client completes the reflection before starting Module 1.1.", "lessonSequence": []}, {"id": "1.1", "stage": 1, "stageName": "Foundation", "name": "Financial Foundations", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to see a clear, current picture of your own financial position and build the systems that make investing possible.", "prerequisites": [], "beforeAfter": {"before": "A vague or anxious sense of your finances.", "after": "You can state your income, spending, savings position, and emergency fund status without needing to check."}, "coachingConnection": "Reflection Opportunity", "completionEvidence": "Written reflection on current financial position, plus a stated goal.", "lessonSequence": ["1.1.1", "1.1.2", "1.1.3", "1.1.4", "1.1.5", "1.1.6"]}, {"id": "1.2", "stage": 1, "stageName": "Foundation", "name": "Investor Mindset", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to recognise the psychological game being played before engaging with any investing mechanics.", "prerequisites": ["1.1"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You want quick results and see investing as prediction.", "after": "You understand investing as a long-term, probabilistic process."}, "coachingConnection": "Reflection Opportunity", "completionEvidence": "Knowledge check on compounding, plus a short reflection on a past financial decision.", "lessonSequence": ["1.2.1", "1.2.2", "1.2.3", "1.2.4", "1.2.5", "1.2.6"]}, {"id": "2.1", "stage": 2, "stageName": "Investor Understanding", "name": "Investing Fundamentals", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to understand what investing actually is and the vehicles available to do it.", "prerequisites": ["1.2"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You conflate investing with speculation, or are not sure what \"owning\" an investment actually means.", "after": "You can explain the difference between stocks, bonds, funds, ETFs, and what index versus active investing means."}, "coachingConnection": "None", "completionEvidence": "Concept explanation exercise -- you explain each vehicle type in your own words.", "lessonSequence": ["2.1.1", "2.1.2", "2.1.3", "2.1.4", "2.1.5", "2.1.6"]}, {"id": "2.2", "stage": 2, "stageName": "Investor Understanding", "name": "UK Investment Wrappers", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to understand what a tax wrapper is, and why the choice of wrapper is separate from the choice of investment.", "prerequisites": ["2.1"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You do not distinguish between the investment itself and the wrapper it is held in, or assume wrappers are interchangeable.", "after": "You understand the general purpose and category of each main UK wrapper type, and know to check the Client Hub for current rules before acting."}, "coachingConnection": "Personal Context Required", "completionEvidence": "Knowledge check -- you correctly match each wrapper type to its general category and purpose, without being tested on any specific figure.", "lessonSequence": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4"]}, {"id": "2.3", "stage": 2, "stageName": "Investor Understanding", "name": "How Markets Work", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to understand markets as a mechanism, not a mysterious force, by explaining how prices form and why they move.", "prerequisites": ["2.1"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You see markets as unpredictable systems driven by news.", "after": "You can explain why reacting to short-term price movement often leads to poor decisions."}, "coachingConnection": "None", "completionEvidence": "Concept explanation -- you explain, in your own words, why a price might move on a given day, and why acting on that kind of short-term movement often leads to poor decisions.", "lessonSequence": ["2.3.1", "2.3.2", "2.3.3", "2.3.4", "2.3.5", "2.3.6"]}, {"id": "2.4", "stage": 2, "stageName": "Investor Understanding", "name": "Businesses Create Value", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to see an investment as ownership of something real, not just a price on a screen.", "prerequisites": ["2.1"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You think of shares primarily as price movements.", "after": "You understand a share as an ownership claim on a real business that generates revenue and profit."}, "coachingConnection": "None", "completionEvidence": "Concept explanation -- you explain what \"owning a share\" actually means, in your own words.", "lessonSequence": ["2.4.1", "2.4.2", "2.4.3", "2.4.4"]}, {"id": "2.5", "stage": 2, "stageName": "Investor Understanding", "name": "Risk, Time and Diversification", "type": "module", "purpose": "Develops the ability to reason about risk and uncertainty as central to investing, not a side detail.", "prerequisites": ["2.1"], "beforeAfter": {"before": "You see \"risk\" as something to avoid entirely.", "after": "You understand risk and volatility as distinct, and diversification and time horizon as tools for managing, not eliminating, risk."}, "coachingConnection": "Personal Context Required", "completionEvidence": "Scenario exercise -- you reason through a hypothetical risk trade-off, correctly applying at least two of the module's three tools.", "lessonSequence": ["2.5.1", "2.5.2", "2.5.3", "2.5.4"]}],
  ASSETS: {"1.1.1": [{"file": "assets/1_1_1_Money_Mindset_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "example", "replacesText": true, "scenarios": [{"tag": "Money as fragile", "text": "Bills paid carefully, treats rare, a general sense things could go wrong. As an adult, saving comes naturally, almost compulsively -- but spending on themselves feels uncomfortable, even when they can genuinely afford it."}, {"tag": "Money rarely discussed", "text": "Simply available when needed, with little conversation around it. As an adult, spending is easy and saving is harder -- not from carelessness, but because nothing in their early experience taught them saving mattered."}], "title": "Your money habits were learned, not chosen."}], "1.1.2": [{"file": "assets/1_1_2_Personal_Financial_Position_WorkedExample.html", "kind": "WorkedExample", "step": "example", "moneyRows": {"title": "An honest rough picture beats a perfect one you keep avoiding.", "sub": "One illustrative month, written down honestly rather than estimated.", "rows": [{"name": "Income", "desc": "Take-home pay, this month", "amt": "2,400"}, {"name": "Essential spending", "desc": "Rent, bills, food, transport", "amt": "1,750"}, {"name": "Discretionary spending", "desc": "Everything else that went out", "amt": "450"}], "total": {"name": "Left over", "amt": "200"}, "footerNote": "This is a snapshot, not a verdict. One unusual month can distort the picture — the value is in doing this honestly and repeating it, not in getting it perfect once."}, "replacesText": true, "sliders": {"sliders": [{"id": "income", "label": "Income -- take-home pay, this month", "min": 1000, "max": 5000, "step": 50, "default": 2400}, {"id": "essential", "label": "Essential spending -- rent, bills, food, transport", "min": 500, "max": 4000, "step": 50, "default": 1750}, {"id": "discretionary", "label": "Discretionary spending -- everything else", "min": 0, "max": 1500, "step": 25, "default": 450}], "totalLabel": "Left over", "hint": "Move the sliders to try your own rough numbers. Nothing here is saved or sent anywhere -- this is just to see how the picture shifts."}}], "1.1.3": [{"file": "assets/1_1_3_Income_Spending_Saving_Systems_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.1.3 Income, Spending, Saving Systems -- Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n  --risk:#7A3541;--risk-tint:#F5EBEC;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\nsvg text{font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.framework-tag{font-size:13px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:-16px 0 24px;}\n.bills-label{font-size:13px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin:20px 0 10px;}\n.bills-row{display:flex;gap:10px;}\n.bill-chip{flex:1;text-align:center;padding:12px 10px;border-radius:8px;background:var(--navy-tint);border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);transition:all .2s;}\n.bill-chip.highlight{background:var(--gold-light);border-color:var(--gold);color:#5c481a;font-weight:600;}\n.bill-chip.faded{background:var(--cream);border-color:var(--cream-dark);color:#a9b2bf;text-decoration:line-through;}\n.toggle-row{display:flex;gap:8px;margin-bottom:24px;}\n.toggle-btn{flex:1;padding:11px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1.5px solid var(--cream-dark);background:#fff;color:var(--navy-mid);font-size:13.5px;font-weight:500;font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;cursor:pointer;transition:all .15s;}\n.toggle-btn.active{background:var(--navy);border-color:var(--navy);color:#fff;font-weight:600;}\n.view{display:none;}\n.view.active{display:block;}\n.interactive-hint{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:14px;line-height:1.55;font-style:italic;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>Systems beat willpower.</h1>\n  <p class=\"framework-tag\">Known as \"Pay Yourself First\" -- a long-standing principle in personal finance.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"toggle-row\">\n    <button class=\"toggle-btn active\" id=\"btnSystem\" onclick=\"setMode('system')\">With a system</button>\n    <button class=\"toggle-btn\" id=\"btnManual\" onclick=\"setMode('manual')\">Without one</button>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"view active\" id=\"viewSystem\">\n    <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n      <defs>\n        <marker id=\"arrowGood\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n          <path d=\"M2 1L8 5L2 9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n        </marker>\n      </defs>\n      <rect x=\"20\" y=\"60\" width=\"120\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Payday</text>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Income arrives</text>\n      <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"178\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#arrowGood)\"/>\n      <rect x=\"180\" y=\"60\" width=\"230\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n      <text x=\"295\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Pay yourself first</text>\n      <text x=\"295\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Treated like a bill, not a choice</text>\n      <line x1=\"410\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#arrowGood)\"/>\n      <rect x=\"452\" y=\"60\" width=\"180\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n      <text x=\"542\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Saved, before it is seen</text>\n      <text x=\"542\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">What is left is what is spent</text>\n    </svg>\n    <p class=\"interactive-hint\">Saving or investing is committed the same day, treated with the same weight as any other bill on the list, not a discretionary choice made after the rest.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"view\" id=\"viewManual\">\n    <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n      <rect x=\"20\" y=\"60\" width=\"120\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Payday</text>\n      <text x=\"80\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Income arrives</text>\n      <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"178\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n      <rect x=\"180\" y=\"60\" width=\"230\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#F7F4ED\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"295\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#6b7280\">A month of spending</text>\n      <text x=\"295\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">Decided case by case</text>\n      <line x1=\"410\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n      <rect x=\"452\" y=\"60\" width=\"180\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#F5EBEC\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n      <text x=\"542\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#7A3541\">Whatever is left</text>\n      <text x=\"542\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A3541\">Often, not much</text>\n    </svg>\n    <p class=\"interactive-hint\">Every spending decision is made first. Saving or investing is not on the committed list at all, it only gets whatever survives everything else.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"bills-label\">Treated as equally non-negotiable as any other committed expense:</p>\n  <div class=\"bills-row\" id=\"billsRow\">\n    <div class=\"bill-chip\">Rent</div>\n    <div class=\"bill-chip\">Utilities</div>\n    <div class=\"bill-chip highlight\" id=\"billMoney\">Saving / Investing</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">The mechanism is ordering, not effort: saving or investing happens first and automatically, treated as a committed line item like a bill, not a discretionary choice made after everything else.</p>\n</div>\n<script>\nfunction setMode(mode) {\n  document.getElementById('btnSystem').classList.toggle('active', mode === 'system');\n  document.getElementById('btnManual').classList.toggle('active', mode === 'manual');\n  document.getElementById('viewSystem').classList.toggle('active', mode === 'system');\n  document.getElementById('viewManual').classList.toggle('active', mode === 'manual');\n  var chip = document.getElementById('billMoney');\n  chip.classList.toggle('highlight', mode === 'system');\n  chip.classList.toggle('faded', mode === 'manual');\n}\n<\/script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "1.1.4": [{"file": "assets/1_1_4_Emergency_Funds_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.1.4 Emergency Funds -- Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.pull-quote{border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:4px 0 4px 20px;margin:24px 0 0;}\n.pull-quote p{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-style:italic;font-size:17px;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.4;}\n.pull-quote .attr{font-size:12px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:normal;}\n.slider-row{margin-top:20px;}\n.slider-row .srow-top{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;margin-bottom:8px;}\n.slider-row .lbl{font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);}\n.slider-row .val{font-size:16px;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy);white-space:nowrap;}\ninput[type=\"range\"]{-webkit-appearance:none;width:100%;height:4px;border-radius:2px;background:var(--cream-dark);outline:none;display:block;}\ninput[type=\"range\"]::-webkit-slider-thumb{-webkit-appearance:none;width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--gold);cursor:pointer;border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--cream-dark);}\ninput[type=\"range\"]::-moz-range-thumb{width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--gold);cursor:pointer;border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--cream-dark);}\n.interactive-hint{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:12px;line-height:1.55;font-style:italic;min-height:2.4em;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>An emergency fund protects your investments from being sold at the wrong moment.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 280\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <defs>\n      <clipPath id=\"shieldClip\">\n        <path d=\"M150 40 C150 40 110 55 90 60 C90 130 100 175 150 200 C200 175 210 130 210 60 C190 55 150 40 150 40 Z\"/>\n      </clipPath>\n    </defs>\n\n    <!-- shield: emergency fund, empty outline -->\n    <path d=\"M150 40 C150 40 110 55 90 60 C90 130 100 175 150 200 C200 175 210 130 210 60 C190 55 150 40 150 40 Z\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n    <!-- fill level, clipped to the shield's own shape, driven by the slider -->\n    <rect id=\"fund-fill\" x=\"85\" y=\"200\" width=\"130\" height=\"0\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" clip-path=\"url(#shieldClip)\"/>\n\n    <path d=\"M150 40 C150 40 110 55 90 60 C90 130 100 175 150 200 C200 175 210 130 210 60 C190 55 150 40 150 40 Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Emergency</text>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">fund</text>\n\n    <!-- growing plant: investments, protected, undisturbed -->\n    <rect x=\"380\" y=\"180\" width=\"90\" height=\"55\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"425\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Investments</text>\n    <line x1=\"425\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#639922\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"410\" cy=\"115\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#97C459\" transform=\"rotate(-25 410 115)\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"440\" cy=\"115\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#97C459\" transform=\"rotate(25 440 115)\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"425\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#639922\"/>\n\n    <!-- storm arrows deflected by shield, not reaching the plant -->\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A3541\">unexpected</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7A3541\">costs</text>\n\n    <!-- caption under shield -->\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">absorbs the shock</text>\n    <text x=\"425\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">stays invested, undisturbed</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <div class=\"slider-row\">\n    <div class=\"srow-top\"><span class=\"lbl\">Months of essential spending set aside</span><span class=\"val\" id=\"v-months\">0</span></div>\n    <input type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"6\" step=\"0.5\" value=\"0\" oninput=\"updateFund()\" id=\"s-months\">\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"interactive-hint\" id=\"fund-hint\">With nothing set aside, any unexpected cost has to come from debt or from selling something else.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"pull-quote\">\n    <p>\"The investor's chief problem -- and even his worst enemy -- is likely to be himself.\"</p>\n    <span class=\"attr\">-- Benjamin Graham, The Intelligent Investor</span>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">There is no correct number of months to reach here. Only what feels like enough disruption you could absorb without selling anything.</p>\n</div>\n<script>\nfunction updateFund() {\n  var monthsEl = document.getElementById('s-months');\n  var months = +monthsEl.value;\n  document.getElementById('v-months').textContent = months;\n\n  var maxFillHeight = 160; // spans the shield's usable interior, y:200 up to y:40\n  var h = Math.min(maxFillHeight, (months / 6) * maxFillHeight);\n  var fill = document.getElementById('fund-fill');\n  fill.setAttribute('height', h);\n  fill.setAttribute('y', 200 - h);\n  fill.setAttribute('fill', months >= 3 ? '#C0DD97' : (months > 0 ? '#FAC775' : '#EDF2F8'));\n\n  var hint = document.getElementById('fund-hint');\n  if (months === 0) {\n    hint.textContent = 'With nothing set aside, any unexpected cost has to come from debt or from selling something else.';\n  } else if (months < 3) {\n    hint.textContent = 'This would absorb a smaller shock, a repair, a short gap, but not an extended one.';\n  } else {\n    hint.textContent = 'This would absorb a more serious disruption, such as a period without income. There is still no single correct number to reach.';\n  }\n}\n<\/script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "1.1.5": [{"file": "assets/1_1_5_Financial_Goals_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "reflection", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.1.5 Financial Goals — Reflection Card</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:640px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:26px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.scale{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;margin-bottom:28px;}\n.item{border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;font-size:14px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;}\n.vague{background:#F7F4ED;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);color:#6b7280;}\n.specific{background:var(--navy-tint);color:var(--navy);}\n.item .cat{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.15em;font-weight:600;}\n.divider{border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--cream-dark);margin:28px 0;}\n.stems{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;}\n.stems li{background:var(--gold-light);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 20px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;margin-bottom:12px;font-style:italic;color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.stems li:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Reflection</p>\n  <h1>A vague goal produces vague decisions.</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"scale\">\n    <div class=\"item vague\"><span>\"Grow my money\"</span><span class=\"cat\">Vague</span></div>\n    <div class=\"item specific\"><span>\"A deposit for a home in three years\"</span><span class=\"cat\">Specific</span></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <hr class=\"divider\">\n\n  <ul class=\"stems\">\n    <li>What is one thing you are saving or investing toward, specifically?</li>\n    <li>How would you know if you had achieved it?</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Goals change, and that is normal. Stating one now is not a binding commitment -- it is a starting point specific enough to act on.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "1.1.6": [{"file": "assets/1_1_6_Why_Investing_Matters_WorkedExample.html", "kind": "WorkedExample", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.1.6 Why Investing Matters -- Worked Example</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--navy);}\n.sub{font-size:14px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin:0 0 28px;}\nsvg text{font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:20px;}\n.slider-row{margin-top:20px;}\n.slider-row .srow-top{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;margin-bottom:8px;}\n.slider-row .lbl{font-size:14px;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);}\n.slider-row .val{font-size:16px;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy);white-space:nowrap;}\ninput[type=\"range\"]{-webkit-appearance:none;width:100%;height:4px;border-radius:2px;background:var(--cream-dark);outline:none;display:block;}\ninput[type=\"range\"]::-webkit-slider-thumb{-webkit-appearance:none;width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--gold);cursor:pointer;border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--cream-dark);}\ninput[type=\"range\"]::-moz-range-thumb{width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--gold);cursor:pointer;border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 0 0 1px var(--cream-dark);}\n.interactive-hint{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:10px;line-height:1.55;font-style:italic;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Worked example</p>\n  <h1>Saving preserves money; investing gives it room to grow.</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Illustrative only -- a simple, hypothetical contrast over a long horizon, not a projection or a promise.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"580\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <rect id=\"cash-bar\" x=\"120\" y=\"142\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#F5EBEC\" stroke=\"#7A3541\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Cash, sitting still</text>\n    <rect id=\"invested-bar\" x=\"390\" y=\"132\" width=\"90\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"435\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Invested over time</text>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#7A3541\">£1,000 &#8594; <tspan id=\"v-cash-amt\">£863</tspan></text>\n    <text x=\"435\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">£1,000 &#8594; <tspan id=\"v-invested-amt\">£1,338</tspan></text>\n    <text x=\"165\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#7A3541\">real value, after inflation</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <div class=\"slider-row\">\n    <div class=\"srow-top\"><span class=\"lbl\">Years, illustrative</span><span class=\"val\" id=\"v-years\">5</span></div>\n    <input type=\"range\" min=\"1\" max=\"30\" step=\"1\" value=\"5\" oninput=\"updateHorizon()\" id=\"s-years\">\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"interactive-hint\">The cash figure shrinks in real terms as inflation erodes it. The number in the account never falls, but what it can buy does.</p>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">*Illustrative growth rate only, not a real or projected return. Actual returns vary and can fall as well as rise. Cash held over time loses real purchasing power to inflation even though the number in the account never falls, shown here shrinking to reflect that. Investing carries its own real risk of loss, including short-term declines, that full conversation belongs to Stage 2.</p>\n</div>\n<script>\nfunction updateHorizon() {\n  var yEl = document.getElementById('s-years');\n  var years = +yEl.value;\n  document.getElementById('v-years').textContent = years;\n\n  // One shared GBP-to-pixel scale for both bars, anchored so the invested\n  // bar reaches its max height at the 30-year ceiling, keeping both bars\n  // honestly comparable at every point on the slider.\n  var maxInvestedAt30 = 1000 * Math.pow(1.06, 30);\n  var maxBarHeight = 120;\n  var scale = maxBarHeight / maxInvestedAt30;\n  var axisY = 160;\n  var minBarHeight = 4;\n\n  var invested = Math.round(1000 * Math.pow(1.06, years));\n  document.getElementById('v-invested-amt').textContent = '£' + invested.toLocaleString();\n  var investedHeight = Math.max(minBarHeight, invested * scale);\n  var bar = document.getElementById('invested-bar');\n  bar.setAttribute('height', investedHeight);\n  bar.setAttribute('y', axisY - investedHeight);\n\n  var cashReal = Math.round(1000 / Math.pow(1.03, years));\n  document.getElementById('v-cash-amt').textContent = '£' + cashReal.toLocaleString();\n  var cashHeight = Math.max(minBarHeight, cashReal * scale);\n  var cashBar = document.getElementById('cash-bar');\n  cashBar.setAttribute('height', cashHeight);\n  cashBar.setAttribute('y', axisY - cashHeight);\n}\n<\/script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "1.2.1": [{"file": "assets/1_2_1_Long_Term_Thinking_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.2.1 Long-Term Thinking -- Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--navy);}\n.illustrative{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 24px;}\nsvg{display:block;}\n.legend{display:flex;gap:20px;justify-content:center;margin:22px 0 0;flex-wrap:wrap;}\n.legend-item{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.legend-dot{width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;flex-shrink:0;}\n.legend-dot.reactive{background:#8a8f99;}\n.legend-dot.disciplined{background:#C9A84C;border-radius:2px;}\n.legend-dot.conviction{background:#C9A84C;}\n.outcome-block{margin-top:24px;padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--cream-dark);}\n.outcome-title{font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:var(--navy-mid);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;}\n.outcome-illustrative{font-size:11px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin-bottom:16px;}\n.outcome-row{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;margin-bottom:12px;}\n.outcome-row:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}\n.outcome-label{width:130px;flex-shrink:0;font-size:13px;font-weight:500;}\n.outcome-bar-track{flex:1;background:var(--cream);border-radius:5px;height:24px;position:relative;}\n.outcome-bar-fill{height:100%;border-radius:5px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:flex-end;padding-right:10px;}\n.outcome-bar-fill.reactive{width:52%;background:#8a8f99;}\n.outcome-bar-fill.disciplined{width:88%;background:#C9A84C;}\n.outcome-bar-fill span{color:#fff;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:22px;}\n.perspective{background:var(--navy-tint);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 20px;margin-top:20px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--navy);}\n.perspective .k{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.13em;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-bottom:8px;}\n.perspective .not-a-rule{font-style:italic;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:8px;display:block;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>Time in the Market &gt; Timing the Market</h1>\n  <p class=\"illustrative\">An illustrative market path, not real or historical data for any actual market.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 280\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- generic market-like path: a long-term rise, with real drawdowns along the way -->\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"610\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <path d=\"M30,170 C70,155 100,190 130,175 C160,160 175,120 200,140 C230,165 250,205 270,195\n             C300,180 320,110 350,95 C380,82 400,130 420,150 C450,175 470,200 490,190\n             C520,175 540,100 570,75 C590,60 600,55 610,50\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n    <path d=\"M30,170 C70,155 100,190 130,175 C160,160 175,120 200,140 C230,165 250,205 270,195\n             C300,180 320,110 350,95 C380,82 400,130 420,150 C450,175 470,200 490,190\n             C520,175 540,100 570,75 C590,60 600,55 610,50\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" opacity=\"0.35\"/>\n\n    <!-- conviction investor: adds near the bottom of each dip, on the curve -->\n    <circle cx=\"255\" cy=\"192\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"475\" cy=\"189\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n\n    <!-- reactive investor: buys near local peaks, sells on the downslope, on the curve -->\n    <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"175\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a8f99\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a8f99\" stroke-width=\"2.25\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#8a8f99\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"445\" cy=\"172\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a8f99\" stroke-width=\"2.25\"/>\n\n    <!-- labelled once each, to establish the pattern without repeating it -->\n    <text x=\"130\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9.5\" fill=\"#6b7280\">buys</text>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"153\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"9.5\" fill=\"#6b7280\">sells</text>\n    <text x=\"255\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">adds</text>\n\n    <!-- pound cost averaging: even, regular contributions regardless of level -->\n    <g>\n      <rect x=\"24\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n      <rect x=\"144\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n      <rect x=\"264\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n      <rect x=\"384\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n      <rect x=\"504\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n      <rect x=\"600\" y=\"254\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    </g>\n  </svg>\n\n  <div class=\"legend\">\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-dot reactive\"></span>Reactive: buys and sells, chasing the moment</div>\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-dot disciplined\"></span>Disciplined: pound cost averaging -- a fixed schedule, regardless of price</div>\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-dot conviction\"></span>Also adds on dips, conviction unchanged</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Neither person has any special insight the other lacks. Same market, same period -- the difference is behaviour, and whether contributions are driven by the price on the day or by a schedule set in advance.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"outcome-block\">\n    <div class=\"outcome-title\">Illustrative outcome, after 10 years</div>\n    <div class=\"outcome-illustrative\">Hypothetical figures only, not a real or projected return for any market or strategy.</div>\n    <div class=\"outcome-row\">\n      <div class=\"outcome-label\">Reactive</div>\n      <div class=\"outcome-bar-track\"><div class=\"outcome-bar-fill reactive\"><span>+34%*</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"outcome-row\">\n      <div class=\"outcome-label\">Disciplined</div>\n      <div class=\"outcome-bar-track\"><div class=\"outcome-bar-fill disciplined\"><span>+81%*</span></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"outcome-illustrative\" style=\"margin:12px 0 0;\">*Illustrative only. The gap comes from being out of the market during recoveries, not from the size of any single decision.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"perspective\">\n    <div class=\"k\">One perspective worth knowing about</div>\n    Some disciplined investors go a step further and choose to add more specifically during a downturn, if their reasons for holding an investment have not changed.\n    <span class=\"not-a-rule\">This is a real perspective some investors hold, not a rule to follow here. Telling a genuine buying opportunity apart from a warning sign is a hard, separate skill -- not covered until How Investors Think About Businesses and Business Analysis at Depth, later in this platform -- and deciding to add on a dip is itself a timing decision, which is exactly what disciplined, schedule-based investing is designed to avoid needing.</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "1.2.2": [{"file": "assets/1_2_2_Common_Behavioural_Mistakes_Table.html", "kind": "Table", "step": "concept", "nativeTable": {"title": "These patterns are normal, not personal failings.", "tableInner": "<tr>\n      <th>Pattern</th>\n      <th>What it looks like</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Chasing performance</td>\n      <td>Seeing an investment do unusually well over a short period, and putting more money in, assuming the trend will continue.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Panic-selling</td>\n      <td>Seeing an investment fall in value and selling immediately, out of a need to do something rather than sit with the discomfort.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Overconfidence</td>\n      <td>Mistaking a run of good decisions for skill, after a period where things simply happened to go well.</td>\n    </tr>", "footerNote": "These are consistent, well-documented patterns in how people generally behave under uncertainty -- not failures of intelligence, and not something that only happens to unsophisticated investors."}, "insertAfter": "example", "ownPage": true}], "1.2.3": [{"file": "assets/1_2_3_Fear_and_Greed_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.2.3 Fear and Greed -- Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--navy);}\n.generic-note{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 26px;}\nsvg{display:block;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>Fear and greed are the two forces most likely to move you off your plan.</h1>\n  <p class=\"generic-note\">A general pattern, at any time -- not a description of current conditions.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 240\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- fulcrum / calm centre -->\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"560\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"310,150 290,190 330,190\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Calm reasoning</text>\n\n    <!-- greed, left, rising asset -->\n    <rect x=\"40\" y=\"30\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Greed</text>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">an investment rising steadily</text>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">\"missing out would be worse</text>\n    <text x=\"140\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">than getting in late\"</text>\n\n    <!-- fear, right, falling asset -->\n    <rect x=\"380\" y=\"30\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"480\" y=\"55\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Fear</text>\n    <text x=\"480\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">an investment falling sharply</text>\n    <text x=\"480\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">\"doing something would feel</text>\n    <text x=\"480\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">better than sitting still\"</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n    <line x1=\"480\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Neither announces itself as fear or greed in the moment. Both feel like practical thinking -- which is exactly what makes them worth learning to recognise deliberately, rather than trusting it will be obvious when it happens.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "1.2.4": [{"file": "assets/1_2_4_Patience_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "example", "replacesText": true, "scenarios": [{"tag": "No structure", "text": "Checks prices daily. Reacts to any downward movement. Cares just as much about the outcome -- simply has not built anything that makes patience the default."}, {"tag": "Built a structure", "text": "Reviews investments once a year, on a set date. Has deliberately reduced the number of moments where fear or greed gets a chance to influence a decision."}], "title": "Patience is a system, not a personality trait."}], "1.2.5": [{"file": "assets/1_2_5_Compounding_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>1.2.5 Compounding -- Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--navy);}\n.illustrative{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 26px;}\nsvg{display:block;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.hub-pointer{margin-top:22px;background:var(--gold-light);border:1.5px solid var(--gold);border-radius:10px;padding:20px 24px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap;}\n.hub-pointer .txt{font-size:13.5px;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.5;}\n.hub-pointer .txt b{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;}\n.hub-pointer .cta{font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:#8a6d1f;white-space:nowrap;padding:10px 18px;background:#fff;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--gold);}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>Compounding rewards time more than timing.</h1>\n  <p class=\"illustrative\">Illustrative figures only, not real or projected returns.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 260\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n    <!-- early starter, stops contributing after a while -->\n    <path d=\"M80,215 C160,205 240,180 320,130 C400,80 460,45 520,25\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"215\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <text x=\"80\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">starts here</text>\n    <circle cx=\"520\" cy=\"25\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <text x=\"490\" y=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">starts earliest, stops adding</text>\n\n    <!-- later starter, keeps contributing longer -->\n    <path d=\"M260,218 C330,212 400,195 460,165 C500,145 520,120 540,95\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"218\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">starts later</text>\n    <circle cx=\"540\" cy=\"95\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <text x=\"510\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">contributes steadily, longer</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Despite contributing far less in total, the earliest starter often ends up with more -- purely because their money has had more time for growth to build on previous growth. The effect is small and unremarkable at first, and only becomes dramatic with enough time behind it.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"hub-pointer\">\n    <span class=\"txt\">Good news -- a tool for exactly this already exists. See how your own numbers look.</span>\n    <span class=\"cta\">Cost of Waiting calculator -- Client Hub &#8594;</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "1.2.6": [{"file": "assets/1_2_6_Probabilistic_Thinking_WorkedExample.html", "kind": "WorkedExample", "step": "example", "answerPair": {"title": "Investing deals in likelihoods, not certainties.", "question": "\"Will this investment be worth more in five years?\"", "answers": [{"style": "prediction", "label": "A prediction-based answer", "text": "\"Yes.\" Or: \"No.\""}, {"style": "probabilistic", "label": "A probabilistic answer", "text": "\"There is a reasonable chance of meaningful growth, a smaller but real chance of a poor outcome, and a wide range of plausible results in between, depending on conditions that cannot be known in advance.\""}], "footerNotes": ["The second answer is less satisfying to hear. It is also considerably more honest, and closer to how experienced, disciplined investors actually think, even when they do not say it out loud in those exact terms.", "It is also part of why many investors choose to focus more on the underlying business than on the share price day to day: the business is something you can actually reason about, while the price on any given day is only one point in a much wider range of what it could plausibly be."]}, "replacesText": true}], "2.1.1": [{"file": "assets/2_1_1_What_Investing_Is_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "example", "replacesText": true, "scenarios": [{"tag": "An examinable basis", "text": "Has looked at what the company does, how it earns money, and believes it will likely continue operating profitably. A reason that could be discussed, challenged, and revisited if circumstances changed."}, {"tag": "No examinable basis", "text": "No view on any of that. Putting money in purely because the price has been rising and expecting it to keep rising. A bet on momentum continuing, nothing more."}], "title": "Investing is deploying capital with a reasonable basis for return -- not a bet."}], "2.1.2": [{"file": "assets/2_1_2_Stocks_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.1.2 Stocks — Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>A share is a claim on a real business, not just a price.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 280\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- left: the price, a number on a screen -->\n    <rect x=\"30\" y=\"90\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#F7F4ED\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">Ticker</text>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#6b7280\">£4.82</text>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">a number that moves</text>\n\n    <!-- arrow linking price to the real thing -->\n    <line x1=\"212\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"268\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"240\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">a claim on</text>\n\n    <!-- right: the real business, ownership -->\n    <rect x=\"270\" y=\"60\" width=\"300\" height=\"160\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"420\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">A furniture company</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"290\" y=\"105\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"350\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Factories</text>\n    <text x=\"350\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#6b7280\">real operations</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"430\" y=\"105\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"490\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Brand</text>\n    <text x=\"490\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#6b7280\">real reputation</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"290\" y=\"165\" width=\"260\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"420\" y=\"189\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Future profits or losses</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">If the company sells more furniture and becomes more profitable over time, the small stake behind that ticker is, in principle, worth more -- because the business itself is worth more, not because the number moved.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.1.3": [{"file": "assets/2_1_3_Bonds_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.1.3 Bonds — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n  --risk:#7A3541;--risk-tint:#F5EBEC;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 10px;color:var(--navy);}\n.sub{font-size:14px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin:0 0 26px;line-height:1.5;}\n.risks-label{font-size:13px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin:22px 0 10px;}\n.risks-row{display:flex;gap:12px;}\n.risk-chip{flex:1;background:var(--risk-tint);border:1px solid var(--risk);border-radius:10px;padding:14px 16px;}\n.risk-chip .name{font-size:12.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--risk);margin-bottom:4px;}\n.risk-chip .desc{font-size:12.5px;color:#5c2b32;line-height:1.5;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:22px;}\n@media (max-width:560px){.risks-row{flex-direction:column;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>A bond is a loan, not ownership.</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Read left to right, like a timeline: what you hand over, what you get back, and when.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 680 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- the timeline itself -->\n    <line x1=\"90\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"590\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n\n    <!-- Today: lend the principal -->\n    <circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"160\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Today</text>\n    <rect x=\"6\" y=\"180\" width=\"174\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">You lend the</text>\n    <text x=\"90\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">issuer money</text>\n\n    <!-- Coupon: interest, repeated -->\n    <circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"160\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"160\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"160\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <text x=\"290\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">Coupon</text>\n    <rect x=\"185\" y=\"180\" width=\"210\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"290\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">Regular interest</text>\n    <text x=\"290\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">payments, repeated</text>\n\n    <!-- Maturity: principal returned -->\n    <circle cx=\"560\" cy=\"160\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <text x=\"560\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Maturity</text>\n    <rect x=\"460\" y=\"180\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"560\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Your original</text>\n    <text x=\"560\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">principal is returned</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"risks-label\">Two risks worth holding in mind before treating any bond as risk-free:</p>\n  <div class=\"risks-row\">\n    <div class=\"risk-chip\">\n      <div class=\"name\">Credit risk</div>\n      <div class=\"desc\">The chance the issuer cannot actually repay what it owes.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"risk-chip\">\n      <div class=\"name\">Interest-rate risk</div>\n      <div class=\"desc\">A bond's value can fall if new bonds start offering higher interest than it does.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">At no point in this timeline do you own any part of the issuer. You are simply owed money by them, and the promise behind it is only as strong as their actual ability to keep it.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.1.4": [{"file": "assets/2_1_4_Funds_and_ETFs_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.1.4 Funds and ETFs — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>A fund pools many holdings into one investment.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 240\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <defs>\n      <marker id=\"af\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n        <path d=\"M2 1L8 5L2 9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      </marker>\n    </defs>\n\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"90\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"85\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">You</text>\n    <text x=\"85\" y=\"134\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">One purchase</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"152\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"208\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#af)\"/>\n\n    <rect x=\"210\" y=\"70\" width=\"190\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"305\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Fund</text>\n    <text x=\"305\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">a container, not</text>\n    <text x=\"305\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">a separate asset class</text>\n    <text x=\"305\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">holds many, in agreed proportions</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"402\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"458\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#af)\"/>\n\n    <g>\n      <rect x=\"460\" y=\"55\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"490\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Share</text>\n      <rect x=\"530\" y=\"55\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"560\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Share</text>\n      <rect x=\"460\" y=\"97\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"490\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Bond</text>\n      <rect x=\"530\" y=\"97\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"560\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Share</text>\n      <rect x=\"460\" y=\"139\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"490\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Bond</text>\n      <rect x=\"530\" y=\"139\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.6\"/>\n      <text x=\"560\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">and more</text>\n    </g>\n\n    <text x=\"525\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b7280\">many individual holdings underneath</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">The label does not do the diversifying -- the actual composition does. A narrow, sector-specific fund can be just as concentrated as owning a small handful of shares directly.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.1.5": [{"file": "assets/2_1_5_Index_vs_Active_Table.html", "kind": "Table", "step": "concept", "nativeTable": {"title": "Tracking a market and trying to beat it are two different goals.", "tableInner": "<tr>\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Index approach</th>\n      <th>Active approach</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Goal</td>\n      <td class=\"col-index\">Track a defined part of the market, rising and falling roughly in line with it.</td>\n      <td class=\"col-active\">Deliberately select or adjust holdings, aiming to do better than a straightforward market average.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>How holdings are chosen</td>\n      <td class=\"col-index\">Follows the composition of the market it tracks, without individual judgement calls.</td>\n      <td class=\"col-active\">Chosen and adjusted by a manager, based on their own judgement.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>What it assumes</td>\n      <td class=\"col-index\">Matching the market's average outcome is a reasonable, defensible goal on its own.</td>\n      <td class=\"col-active\">Skilled selection can identify a better outcome than the market average.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Whether one is \"right\"</td>\n      <td class=\"col-neutral\" colspan=\"2\">Neither. Both are coherent, defensible approaches built on different assumptions about markets.</td>\n    </tr>", "footerNote": "Whether active management tends to outperform index approaches over time is a genuinely contested, well-studied question -- not a settled one, and not resolved here."}, "insertAfter": "example", "ownPage": true}], "2.1.6": [{"file": "assets/2_1_6_Asset_Ownership_WorkedExample.html", "kind": "WorkedExample", "step": "example", "tagRows": {"title": "Every vehicle here is a different route to ownership or lending.", "sub": "One core question, asked of a mixed collection of holdings.", "q": "\"Am I lending money here, or do I own a piece of something real?\"", "rows": [{"label": "An individual share", "tagClass": "own", "tag": "Ownership"}, {"label": "A government bond", "tagClass": "lend", "tag": "Lending"}, {"label": "A fund built mostly of shares", "tagClass": "own", "tag": "Ownership"}, {"label": "A fund built mostly of bonds", "tagClass": "lend", "tag": "Lending"}, {"label": "A fund holding both shares and bonds", "tagClass": "both", "tag": "Both, in combination"}], "footerNote": "This single question, asked consistently, cuts through a surprising amount of confusion that builds up when vehicles are described only by their names -- ISAs, funds, ETFs -- without reference to what is actually happening underneath each one."}, "replacesText": true}], "2.2.1": [{"file": "assets/2_2_1_What_a_Wrapper_Means_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.2.1 What a Wrapper Means — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.no-figures{font-size:11px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin-top:6px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>A wrapper is the container, not the investment.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 260\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- the same fund, top -->\n    <rect x=\"240\" y=\"15\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"330\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">The same fund</text>\n    <text x=\"330\" y=\"53\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">identical holdings, either way</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n    <line x1=\"370\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n\n    <!-- wrapper 1: tax-advantaged container -->\n    <rect x=\"30\" y=\"105\" width=\"240\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Wrapper: tax-advantaged</text>\n    <rect x=\"60\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"171\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Same fund inside</text>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">growth sheltered from certain tax</text>\n\n    <!-- wrapper 2: no special treatment -->\n    <rect x=\"390\" y=\"105\" width=\"240\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"510\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Wrapper: no special treatment</text>\n    <rect x=\"420\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"510\" y=\"171\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Same fund inside</text>\n    <text x=\"510\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">ordinary tax treatment applies</text>\n  </svg>\n  <p class=\"no-figures\">Category and purpose only -- no figures, thresholds, or dates. Current rules live on the Client Hub.</p>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">What differs between the two is entirely how any growth is treated for tax. Nothing about the investment itself changes -- same person, same holdings, same fund.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "2.2.2": [{"file": "assets/2_2_2_General_Purpose_Table.html", "kind": "Table", "step": "concept", "nativeTable": {"title": "Different wrappers exist for different purposes, not interchangeable labels.", "tableInner": "<tr>\n      <th>Wrapper</th>\n      <th>General category</th>\n      <th>Typically suits</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Stocks and Shares ISA</td>\n      <td>Tax-advantaged saving</td>\n      <td>Medium to longer-term goals. Growth is sheltered from income tax and capital gains tax while held inside the wrapper.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>SIPP / Pension</td>\n      <td>Long-term retirement saving</td>\n      <td>Its own distinct tax treatment, and its own distinct rules on when the money can actually be accessed.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>General Investment Account</td>\n      <td>No special tax treatment</td>\n      <td>Typically used once other wrapper allowances have been used, or where flexibility matters more than tax treatment.</td>\n    </tr>", "footerNote": "Each wrapper is built around a genuinely different purpose. Which one, or which combination, suits your own circumstances is a personal question, best explored with a coach."}, "insertAfter": "example", "ownPage": true}], "2.2.3": [{"file": "assets/2_2_3_Wrapper_vs_Investment_Choice_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "example", "replacesText": true, "scenarios": [{"tag": "What to invest in", "text": "A decision about a particular fund or holding. On its own, this says nothing yet about which wrapper it should sit inside."}, {"tag": "Where to hold it", "text": "A decision about which wrapper suits your goals. Made independently of which specific fund or share eventually goes inside it."}], "title": "Choosing where to hold an investment is separate from choosing what to hold."}], "2.3.1": [{"file": "assets/2_3_1_What_Is_a_Market_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.3.1 What Is a Market? — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>A market is a mechanism, not a mystery.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 260\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- buyers, left -->\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Many buyers</text>\n    <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"50\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"65\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"85\" cy=\"95\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"100\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n    <!-- sellers, right -->\n    <text x=\"510\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">Many sellers</text>\n    <circle cx=\"460\" cy=\"60\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"510\" cy=\"50\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"560\" cy=\"65\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"480\" cy=\"95\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"535\" cy=\"100\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n    <!-- lines converging toward the centre, no single decision-maker -->\n    <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <line x1=\"480\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <line x1=\"535\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <line x1=\"460\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n\n    <!-- the emergent price, centre -->\n    <rect x=\"230\" y=\"170\" width=\"160\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"/>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">A price</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#6b7280\">emerges from the interaction</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">No single person or institution sets the price directly. It emerges from many individual decisions happening at once -- which is what makes a market a mechanism, not a single decision-maker with a view of its own.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "2.3.2": [{"file": "assets/2_3_2_Buyers_Sellers_Liquidity_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.3.2 Buyers, Sellers, and Liquidity — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 28px;}\n.title{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:24px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px;}\n.panel{border-radius:10px;padding:20px 22px;}\n.panel.liquid{background:var(--navy-tint);}\n.panel.illiquid{background:var(--gold-light);}\n.panel .tag{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px;}\n.liquid .tag{color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.illiquid .tag{color:#8a6d1f;}\n.dots{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-bottom:14px;}\n.dot{width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;}\n.liquid .dot{background:#1D3557;}\n.illiquid .dot{background:#C9A84C;}\n.desc{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n@media (max-width:520px){.pair{grid-template-columns:1fr;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <p class=\"title\">Liquidity is about ease of exit, not stability.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"pair\">\n    <div class=\"panel liquid\">\n      <div class=\"tag\">Highly liquid</div>\n      <div class=\"dots\">\n        <div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div>\n        <div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"desc\">Many buyers and sellers active at once. A trade can usually happen quickly, close to the last agreed price.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"panel illiquid\">\n      <div class=\"tag\">Less liquid</div>\n      <div class=\"dots\">\n        <div class=\"dot\"></div><div class=\"dot\"></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"desc\">Fewer active participants. Finding a buyer or seller may take longer, or require accepting a less favourable price.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">This is genuinely separate from how much a price moves. An asset can be highly liquid and still volatile, or illiquid and relatively stable -- liquidity describes how easily you could get in or out, not how steady the price is.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "example"}], "2.3.3": [{"file": "assets/2_3_3_Price_Discovery_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.3.3 Price Discovery — Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:680px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.pull-quote{border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:4px 0 4px 20px;margin:24px 0 0;}\n.pull-quote p{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-style:italic;font-size:17px;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.4;}\n.pull-quote .attr{font-size:12px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:normal;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>Price is a constantly negotiated estimate, not a hidden truth.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 240\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- a wandering, ongoing negotiation line -- not a fixed point being uncovered -->\n    <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"580\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n\n    <path d=\"M40,130 C100,100 130,150 180,120 C230,90 260,140 310,110 C360,80 400,130 450,105 C500,80 540,120 580,95\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n\n    <!-- bid/offer marks scattered along the path, suggesting many small negotiations -->\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"120\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"140\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"360\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"450\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"540\" cy=\"120\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Not a hidden number, slowly revealed</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">An ongoing negotiation, continuously updated</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <div class=\"pull-quote\">\n    <p>\"Price is what you pay; value is what you get.\"</p>\n    <span class=\"attr\">-- Warren Buffett</span>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">The price you see at any moment is simply where that ongoing negotiation currently sits -- not a finished conclusion anyone has actually reached, and not a settled number waiting to be found.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.3.4": [{"file": "assets/2_3_4_Why_Prices_Move_Table.html", "kind": "Table", "step": "concept", "nativeTable": {"title": "Prices respond to expectations as much as facts.", "tableInner": "<tr>\n      <th>Driver</th>\n      <th>What it means</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>New information</td>\n      <td>Something changes participants' view of an asset's underlying value, and the price shifts as those updated views interact.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Shifting participation</td>\n      <td>The balance of active buyers and sellers changes, which can move a price even without any new information about the asset itself.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Changing expectations</td>\n      <td>Views about the future shift, ahead of anything actually happening yet -- covered in more depth in the next lesson.</td>\n    </tr>", "footerNote": "These drivers interact constantly, and no single one operates in isolation. A price at any moment is the combined result of everything currently shaping how participants view an asset's worth."}, "insertAfter": "example", "ownPage": true}], "2.3.5": [{"file": "assets/2_3_5_The_Role_of_Expectations_WorkedExample.html", "kind": "WorkedExample", "step": "example", "kvRows": {"title": "Markets react to surprises, not just news.", "sub": "A hypothetical business, widely expected to perform poorly over a coming period.", "rows": [{"k": "What was already expected", "v": "A poor result"}, {"k": "What actually happened", "v": "A moderately poor result"}, {"k": "How the price reacted", "v": "Rose"}], "footerNote": "Meaningfully better than the low expectation already built into the price -- even though the result itself was still, by any objective measure, a disappointing one for the business. The price had already adjusted downward in anticipation of something worse."}, "replacesText": true}], "2.3.6": [{"file": "assets/2_3_6_Short_Term_vs_Long_Term_Value_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "example", "replacesText": true, "scenarios": [{"tag": "Reacts to the price", "text": "Sees a short-term price fall and interprets it as clear evidence something is fundamentally wrong with the underlying business. Sells."}, {"tag": "Checks first", "text": "Understands price reflects a constantly shifting collective view, not a settled judgement -- and checks whether anything has genuinely changed before reacting."}], "title": "Reacting to short-term price swings usually costs more than it protects."}], "2.4.1": [{"file": "assets/2_4_1_What_a_Business_Actually_Is_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.4.1 What a Business Actually Is — Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--navy);}\n.fictional-note{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 22px;}\nsvg{display:block;}\n.section-title{font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;color:var(--navy-mid);font-weight:600;margin:28px 0 14px;}\n.benefit-row{display:flex;gap:16px;}\n.benefit-box{flex:1;border-radius:10px;padding:18px 20px;background:var(--navy-tint);}\n.benefit-box.dividend{background:var(--gold-light);border:1.5px solid var(--gold);}\n.benefit-box .k{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;color:var(--navy-mid);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;}\n.benefit-box.dividend .k{color:#8a6d1f;}\n.benefit-box .trigger{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-bottom:10px;line-height:1.5;}\n.benefit-box .outcome{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:15px;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.35;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n@media (max-width:520px){.benefit-row{flex-direction:column;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>A share is ownership in something real.</h1>\n  <p class=\"fictional-note\">Meridian Coffee Roasters -- a fictional business, for illustration only. Picture the kind of large, publicly traded coffee chain with hundreds of locations that most people would recognise, without needing this to be any specific one.</p>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 400\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <defs>\n      <marker id=\"ac1\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n        <path d=\"M2 1L8 5L2 9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      </marker>\n    </defs>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#6b7280\">A normal day, inside the cafe behind the ticker</text>\n\n    <!-- top -->\n    <rect x=\"205\" y=\"40\" width=\"190\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Employees show up</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">real people, at work</text>\n\n    <!-- right -->\n    <rect x=\"395\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"490\" y=\"199\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Coffee and food</text>\n    <text x=\"490\" y=\"217\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">prepared and served</text>\n\n    <!-- bottom right -->\n    <rect x=\"315\" y=\"310\" width=\"190\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"410\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Customers pay</text>\n    <text x=\"410\" y=\"352\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">for something real</text>\n\n    <!-- bottom left -->\n    <rect x=\"95\" y=\"310\" width=\"190\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Expenses paid</text>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"352\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">from revenue, as it comes in</text>\n\n    <!-- left -->\n    <rect x=\"15\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"199\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12.5\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Profit reinvested</text>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"217\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">growth, equipment, R&amp;D</text>\n\n    <!-- connecting loop -->\n    <path d=\"M 300 98 L 460 175\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ac1)\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 490 233 L 440 310\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ac1)\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 315 355 L 285 355\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ac1)\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 155 310 L 130 233\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ac1)\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 130 175 L 260 98\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ac1)\" fill=\"none\"/>\n\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"392\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#6b7280\">This cycle repeats whether or not anyone is watching the share price that day</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"section-title\">And a slice of it is genuinely yours -- in two possible ways</p>\n  <div class=\"benefit-row\">\n    <div class=\"benefit-box\">\n      <div class=\"k\">If the business improves</div>\n      <div class=\"trigger\">More customers, better margins, growth reinvested well over time.</div>\n      <div class=\"outcome\">Your share becomes worth more</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"benefit-box dividend\">\n      <div class=\"k\">If profits are distributed</div>\n      <div class=\"trigger\">Some businesses choose to pay part of their profit out to owners.</div>\n      <div class=\"outcome\">You receive your slice, as a dividend</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Neither is guaranteed. A business can grow without ever paying a dividend, and a share price does not move in lockstep with the business behind it, in the short term. Both are simply real ways the ownership described here can actually reach you.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.4.2": [{"file": "assets/2_4_2_How_Businesses_Create_Value_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.4.2 How Businesses Create Value — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.preview-tag{background:var(--navy-tint);border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);line-height:1.55;margin-top:18px;}\n.preview-tag .k{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:6px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>Value comes from selling for more than it costs, sustainably.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 260\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <defs>\n      <marker id=\"ad2\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n        <path d=\"M2 1L8 5L2 9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n      </marker>\n    </defs>\n\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"170\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"105\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Sells for more</text>\n    <text x=\"105\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">than it costs to deliver</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"192\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"238\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ad2)\"/>\n\n    <rect x=\"240\" y=\"40\" width=\"170\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"325\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Value, once</text>\n    <text x=\"325\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">covers costs, reinvests</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"412\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"458\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" marker-end=\"url(#ad2)\"/>\n\n    <rect x=\"460\" y=\"40\" width=\"180\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"0.75\"/>\n    <text x=\"550\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Sustained, not once</text>\n    <text x=\"550\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">a repeated pattern, not once</text>\n\n    <!-- the loop, showing repetition -->\n    <path d=\"M 550 100 C 550 150 330 150 330 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1.25\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#ad2)\"/>\n    <text x=\"440\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"#6b7280\">repeats, adapting as conditions change</text>\n\n    <!-- the trap: growth without profit -->\n    <line x1=\"330\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"70\" y=\"205\" width=\"520\" height=\"42\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#F7F4ED\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"330\" y=\"231\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"#6b7280\">Growth alone is not this -- a business can grow sales while losing money on every one</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">A business that sells at a profit once is not the same as a business that can keep doing so, year after year, while covering its costs, reinvesting, and adapting.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"preview-tag\">\n    <div class=\"k\">A signpost, not taught here yet</div>\n    What actually drives real value creation, growth acceleration, margin expansion, and the durability of an advantage, is genuinely deeper territory. This lesson gives you the correct starting point; Module 3.2, and later Module 5.2, give it the fuller treatment it deserves.\n  </div>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.4.3": [{"file": "assets/2_4_3_Revenue_Profit_Cash_Table.html", "kind": "Table", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.4.3 Revenue, Profit and Cash — Table</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--navy);}\n.illustrative{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 28px;}\n.term-row{display:flex;gap:22px;align-items:flex-start;padding:20px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--cream-dark);}\n.term-row:last-child{border-bottom:none;}\n.term-name{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:16px;color:var(--navy);width:80px;flex-shrink:0;padding-top:2px;}\n.term-visual{width:150px;flex-shrink:0;}\n.bar-track{background:var(--cream);border-radius:5px;height:26px;position:relative;overflow:hidden;}\n.bar-fill{height:100%;border-radius:5px;}\n.bar-fill.revenue{width:100%;background:var(--navy);}\n.bar-fill.profit{width:34%;background:var(--gold);}\n.cash-visual{height:26px;position:relative;}\n.cash-visual svg{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;}\n.term-body{flex:1;}\n.term-def{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;margin-bottom:8px;}\n.term-confuse{font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--navy-mid);background:var(--navy-tint);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:22px;}\n.combined-label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.15em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:32px 0 16px;border-top:1px solid var(--cream-dark);padding-top:24px;}\n@media (max-width:560px){.term-row{flex-wrap:wrap;} .term-visual{width:100%;order:3;} .term-name{width:auto;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Table</p>\n  <h1>Revenue, profit and cash are three different numbers, not one.</h1>\n  <p class=\"illustrative\">Bars are illustrative proportions only, not real or typical figures for any business.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"term-row\">\n    <div class=\"term-name\">Revenue</div>\n    <div class=\"term-visual\">\n      <div class=\"bar-track\"><div class=\"bar-fill revenue\"></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"term-body\">\n      <div class=\"term-def\">The total amount a business brings in from selling, before anything is taken out for costs.</div>\n      <div class=\"term-confuse\">Mistaken for profit -- enormous revenue can still mean very little profit, or none at all.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"term-row\">\n    <div class=\"term-name\">Profit</div>\n    <div class=\"term-visual\">\n      <div class=\"bar-track\"><div class=\"bar-fill profit\"></div></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"term-body\">\n      <div class=\"term-def\">What remains after the business pays its costs, wages, suppliers, and everything else it takes to operate.</div>\n      <div class=\"term-confuse\">Assumed to equal cash in the bank -- it does not. Profit is a paper measure, not a cash balance.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"term-row\">\n    <div class=\"term-name\">Cash</div>\n    <div class=\"term-visual\">\n      <div class=\"cash-visual\">\n        <svg viewBox=\"0 0 150 26\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\">\n          <path d=\"M0,13 C15,4 30,22 45,13 C60,4 75,22 90,13 C105,4 120,22 135,13 L150,13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n        </svg>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"term-body\">\n      <div class=\"term-def\">The actual money moving in and out of the business, day to day -- it can run ahead of or behind profit, not a fixed share of it.</div>\n      <div class=\"term-confuse\">Assumed to track profit closely -- a business can be profitable on paper and still run short of cash.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Learning to ask \"yes, but what was the profit\" -- and then \"yes, but where is the cash\" -- is a genuinely useful habit to build early. A full working understanding of how to read these properly comes later, in Reading Financial Information.</p>\n\n  <p class=\"combined-label\">All three, together</p>\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 320\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <rect x=\"40\" y=\"20\" width=\"520\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#EDF2F8\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Revenue</text>\n\n    <polygon points=\"40,70 560,70 420,140 180,140\" fill=\"#F7F4ED\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#6b7280\">costs, wages, suppliers taken out</text>\n\n    <rect x=\"180\" y=\"140\" width=\"240\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#FBF4E0\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Profit</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n    <line x1=\"400\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#B4B2A9\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n\n    <rect x=\"180\" y=\"245\" width=\"240\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <path d=\"M195,270 C220,258 240,282 265,270 C290,258 310,282 335,270 C360,258 380,282 405,270\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#6b7280\">not a fixed share -- can run ahead of or behind profit</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"317\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">Cash</text>\n  </svg>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.4.4": [{"file": "assets/2_4_4_Why_Business_Quality_Matters_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "reflection", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.4.4 Why Business Quality Will Matter Later — Reflection Card</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:640px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 24px;color:var(--navy);}\n.preview-note{margin-bottom:26px;}\n.preview-note .k{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.13em;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-bottom:14px;}\n.trait{border-radius:10px;padding:16px 20px;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;}\n.trait:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}\n.trait.minor{background:var(--navy-tint);color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.trait.major{background:var(--gold-light);border:1.5px solid var(--gold);}\n.trait .trait-name{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:4px;}\n.trait.major .trait-name{font-size:16px;}\n.preview-summary{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:14px;}\n.pull-quote{border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:2px 0 2px 16px;margin:14px 0 0;}\n.pull-quote p{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-style:italic;font-size:14.5px;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 6px;line-height:1.4;}\n.pull-quote .attr{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:normal;}\n.divider{border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--cream-dark);margin:26px 0;}\n.stems{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;}\n.stems li{background:var(--gold-light);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 20px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;margin-bottom:12px;font-style:italic;color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.stems li:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Reflection</p>\n  <h1>Not all growth is good growth -- more on that later.</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"preview-note\">\n    <div class=\"k\">A signpost, not a lesson -- three traits worth naming now</div>\n    <div class=\"trait minor\">\n      <div class=\"trait-name\">Durability</div>\n      Some businesses sustain what they do far better than others, year after year.\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"trait minor\">\n      <div class=\"trait-name\">Moat</div>\n      Some have real advantages that are genuinely difficult for a competitor to replicate -- creating pricing power, brand loyalty, and room to operate that rivals cannot easily erode.\n      <div class=\"pull-quote\">\n        <p>\"In business, I look for economic castles protected by unbreachable moats.\"</p>\n        <span class=\"attr\">-- Warren Buffett</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"trait major\">\n      <div class=\"trait-name\">Leadership</div>\n      Some are run by people who make consistently sound decisions with the money the business generates -- and strong leadership is often what builds a moat in the first place, not just what manages one.\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"preview-summary\">Learning to recognise all three is a real skill -- covered properly in How Investors Think About Businesses, and again, at greater depth, in Business Analysis at Depth.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <hr class=\"divider\">\n\n  <ul class=\"stems\">\n    <li>Without trying to build a framework, what is one thing that makes you personally trust that a business is well run, based only on your own experience as a customer or observer?</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">This lesson deliberately does not teach you how to judge quality yet. Naming the question now, without answering it, gives you a signpost for where this thread of thinking is heading.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "context"}], "2.5.1": [{"file": "assets/2_5_1_Risk_vs_Volatility_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.5.1 Risk vs. Volatility — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 28px;}\n.title{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto 1fr 1fr;grid-template-rows:auto 1fr 1fr;gap:2px;}\n.axis-label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;color:var(--navy-mid);font-weight:600;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;text-align:center;padding:8px;}\n.corner{}\n.col-head{background:var(--cream);border-radius:6px 6px 0 0;}\n.row-head{writing-mode:vertical-rl;transform:rotate(180deg);background:var(--cream);border-radius:6px 0 0 6px;}\n.quad{border-radius:8px;padding:16px 18px;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;min-height:100px;}\n.quad .qtitle{font-weight:600;font-size:12.5px;margin-bottom:6px;}\n.q-hv-lr{background:var(--navy-tint);}\n.q-lv-hr{background:var(--cream);border:1.5px solid var(--navy);}\n.q-hv-hr{background:var(--cream);border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);}\n.q-lv-lr{background:var(--gold-light);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n.perspective{background:var(--navy-tint);border-radius:10px;padding:18px 22px;margin-top:22px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--navy);}\n.perspective .k{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.13em;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-bottom:8px;}\n.perspective .not-a-rule{font-style:italic;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-top:10px;}\n@media (max-width:560px){.grid{font-size:11px;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <p class=\"title\">Volatility is movement. Risk is the chance of permanent loss.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"grid\">\n    <div class=\"corner\"></div>\n    <div class=\"axis-label col-head\">Moves a lot</div>\n    <div class=\"axis-label col-head\">Barely moves</div>\n\n    <div class=\"axis-label row-head\">Low chance of lasting loss</div>\n    <div class=\"quad q-hv-lr\"><div class=\"qtitle\">Volatile, but not risky</div>Swings sharply along the way. The movement is temporary and the underlying value holds up over time.</div>\n    <div class=\"quad q-lv-lr\"><div class=\"qtitle\">Steady and low risk</div>Calm on the surface, and genuinely little chance of a lasting, permanent loss underneath.</div>\n\n    <div class=\"axis-label row-head\">High chance of lasting loss</div>\n    <div class=\"quad q-hv-hr\"><div class=\"qtitle\">Volatile and risky</div>Swings sharply, and that movement reflects a genuine chance of lasting loss underneath.</div>\n    <div class=\"quad q-lv-hr\"><div class=\"qtitle\">Calm, but risky</div>Appears steady, while carrying real, hidden risk of permanent loss that the calm surface does not reveal.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">Volatility, on its own, is not something to fear -- it is often simply the price of being invested in something that can grow over time. Risk, the chance of a lasting loss, is what genuinely deserves your attention.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"perspective\">\n    <div class=\"k\">One perspective worth knowing about</div>\n    Some investors take this a step further: if a business's volatility comes from short-term movement rather than a genuine change in its underlying value, a lower price for the same business can look more attractive, not less. This is one reason some investors talk about \"conviction\" -- confidence in their own analysis of a business -- as shaping how they interpret a price drop.\n    <p class=\"not-a-rule\">This is a real perspective some investors hold, not a rule to follow here. Telling temporary volatility apart from a genuine, lasting problem is exactly the judgement this lesson has already flagged as difficult, even for experienced investors -- and the analytical skill to build that kind of conviction responsibly is not covered until Module 3.2 and 5.2.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.5.2": [{"file": "assets/2_5_2_Time_Horizons_Diagram.html", "kind": "Diagram", "step": "concept", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.5.2 Time Horizons — Diagram</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:23px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 28px;color:var(--navy);}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Diagram</p>\n  <h1>A longer time horizon changes what risk means in practice.</h1>\n\n  <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 280\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n    <!-- short horizon -->\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Short horizon</text>\n    <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <path d=\"M20,70 C80,55 130,90 190,68 C230,55 250,110 280,70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#1D3557\"/>\n    <text x=\"280\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">money needed here --</text>\n    <text x=\"280\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#2B4C7E\">a dip has no time to recover</text>\n\n    <!-- long horizon -->\n    <text x=\"20\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#1D3557\">Long horizon</text>\n    <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <path d=\"M20,200 C80,180 130,225 190,195 C260,160 320,235 380,190 C450,150 500,220 560,195 C580,187 590,190 600,185\"\n          fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2B4C7E\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"600\" cy=\"185\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#C9A84C\"/>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">money needed far out here --</text>\n    <text x=\"310\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#8a6d1f\">earlier dips had room to smooth out first</text>\n  </svg>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">This is a general, well-observed historical pattern, not a guarantee about any specific future period. A long time horizon does not remove risk -- it changes how much room there is to absorb volatility along the way.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.5.3": [{"file": "assets/2_5_3_Introduction_to_Diversification_Illustration.html", "kind": "Illustration", "step": "example", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.5.3 An Introduction to Diversification — Illustration</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:700px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 8px;color:var(--navy);}\n.illustrative{font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 26px;}\n.chart-block{margin-bottom:30px;}\n.chart-title{font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:10px;}\n.chart-caption{font-size:11px;color:#6b7280;text-align:center;margin-top:8px;}\nsvg{display:block;}\n.legend{display:flex;gap:22px;justify-content:center;margin:26px 0 4px;flex-wrap:wrap;}\n.legend-item{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;font-size:11.5px;color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.legend-swatch{width:22px;height:0;border-top:2.5px solid var(--navy);flex-shrink:0;}\n.legend-swatch.thin{border-top:1.5px solid var(--gold);}\n.legend-swatch.dashed{border-top:1.5px dashed #8a8f99;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Illustration</p>\n  <h1>Diversification manages risk -- it does not remove it.</h1>\n  <p class=\"illustrative\">Illustrative price paths only, not real or projected values for any holding.</p>\n\n  <div class=\"chart-block\">\n    <div class=\"chart-title\">All in one holding</div>\n    <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 140\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n      <path d=\"M20,70 C90,55 160,75 230,60 C280,52 300,58 320,70 C360,95 420,120 480,124 C540,126 600,126 640,126\"\n            fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"640\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    </svg>\n    <p class=\"chart-caption\">A bad outcome here takes the whole value down with it.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"chart-block\">\n    <div class=\"chart-title\">Spread across many holdings</div>\n    <svg width=\"100%\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 140\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n      <path d=\"M20,55 C120,42 220,58 320,45 C420,32 520,50 640,35\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n      <path d=\"M20,62 C120,52 220,65 320,58 C420,52 520,60 640,48\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n      <path d=\"M20,48 C120,40 220,52 320,44 C420,38 520,44 640,32\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#C9A84C\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n      <path d=\"M20,58 C90,50 160,62 230,54 C280,48 300,52 320,60 C360,80 420,112 480,120 C540,124 600,126 640,126\"\n            fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8a8f99\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n      <path d=\"M20,56 C120,48 220,58 320,52 C400,48 460,58 480,60 C540,58 600,48 640,42\"\n            fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1D3557\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n      <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"640\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"#EDE8DA\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    </svg>\n    <p class=\"chart-caption\">Only one line among many -- the overall portfolio barely moves off course.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"legend\">\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-swatch\"></span>Overall portfolio</div>\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-swatch thin\"></span>Individual holdings</div>\n    <div class=\"legend-item\"><span class=\"legend-swatch dashed\"></span>One holding, badly affected</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">This does not remove risk altogether, and it does not guarantee a good outcome. It reduces the damage any single bad outcome can do to the whole -- a different, more modest claim than \"diversification makes investing safe.\"</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}], "2.5.4": [{"file": "assets/2_5_4_Why_Uncertainty_Cannot_Be_Removed_ReflectionCard.html", "kind": "ReflectionCard", "step": "reflection", "rawHtml": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<title>2.5.4 Why Uncertainty Cannot Be Removed, Only Managed — Reflection Card</title>\n<style>\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');\n:root{\n  --navy:#1D3557;--navy-mid:#2B4C7E;--navy-tint:#EDF2F8;\n  --cream:#F7F4ED;--cream-dark:#EDE8DA;\n  --gold:#C9A84C;--gold-light:#FBF4E0;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box;}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--cream);font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;color:var(--navy);padding:48px 24px;display:flex;justify-content:center;}\n.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--cream-dark);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(29,53,87,0.08);width:100%;max-width:640px;padding:40px 44px;}\n.label{font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.2em;color:var(--gold);font-weight:600;margin:0 0 14px;}\nh1{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:22px;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 24px;color:var(--navy);}\n.tools{display:flex;gap:10px;margin-bottom:26px;}\n.tool{flex:1;background:var(--navy-tint);border-radius:10px;padding:14px 12px;text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:var(--navy-mid);line-height:1.4;}\n.tool .n{font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-weight:700;font-size:16px;color:var(--navy);display:block;margin-bottom:4px;}\n.arrow-note{text-align:center;font-size:12px;color:var(--navy-mid);margin-bottom:26px;font-style:italic;}\n.divider{border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--cream-dark);margin:26px 0;}\n.stems{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;}\n.stems li{background:var(--gold-light);border-radius:10px;padding:16px 20px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;margin-bottom:12px;font-style:italic;color:var(--navy-mid);}\n.stems li:last-child{margin-bottom:0;}\n.footer-note{font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.6;margin-top:24px;}\n@media (max-width:480px){.tools{flex-direction:column;}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"label\">Reflection</p>\n  <h1>No amount of sophistication removes uncertainty -- only manages it.</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"tools\">\n    <div class=\"tool\"><span class=\"n\">1</span>Risk vs. volatility</div>\n    <div class=\"tool\"><span class=\"n\">2</span>Time horizon</div>\n    <div class=\"tool\"><span class=\"n\">3</span>Diversification</div>\n  </div>\n  <p class=\"arrow-note\">Three tools from this module. None of them, alone or together, remove uncertainty.</p>\n\n  <hr class=\"divider\">\n\n  <ul class=\"stems\">\n    <li>Think of a decision you made carefully, with good reasoning, that still did not turn out the way you hoped. Looking back, was the reasoning actually wrong, or did an outcome you could not have known in advance simply happen anyway?</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p class=\"footer-note\">What these tools actually do is change your relationship with uncertainty -- helping you reason about it more clearly and avoid unnecessary risks, not eliminate it. That is a meaningfully different, and more honest, goal.</p>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n", "insertAfter": "context"}]}
};
